This whole thread amuses me greatly. I finnished high school in 1987
with more programming experience than a mid 90's college graduate, and
yes, all that experience was in a very un-disciplined environment. The
college courses taught me some discipline and the ability to write more
structured code. We also learned how to analyze the efficiency of
algorithms, but never anything about how to devise one for a task you
can't read about in a book! Fortunately I learned that stuff by NOT
having anyone tell me how things are "supposed to be done". EX: I managed
to derive a line draw routine equivalent to bresenhams at about 10 years
old :-) the "derivation" was simply good optimization of the first
horrible method I could think of that was all integer :-) I was impressed
later when I heard it was actually named after someone. Today I have
perspective-correct-per-pixel texture mapping in mind *without* the
divide per pixel. I still need to write that one up...

: The best that can be said is that this experience may teach
: the need for more structured programming environments.

I was inspired by the idea of making games. People in school do very
uninteresting homework and hence, do not write more than they have to.
Game programming is both challenging and FUN. Also, just about any
algorithm around can be used effectively in a game - it might not be
a game you like, but someone does. Examples include:

Sorts: no one cares about sorting numbers or strings. Sorting polygons for
a rendering engine is much more fun. Heck Z-sorting sprites is fun too.

Trees: No student cares about trees, but try to write a chess program...

Binary trees: We used them in school for sorting a-la heap sort. That's nice
and can apply to sorting for painters alg again, but they are also the basis
for BSP trees which have applications including rendering.

The list could go on. Education needs to cover this type of stuff, but it
also needs to be fun and interesting. I hope to write a kids book on
programming one of these days so that we might have more people that know
what they're doing :-) Don't compain if Visual Basic is the language of
choice, I won't be using 95% of the "features". I need to do this soon,
before I completely forget how I thought as a kid - fortunately I have
a nephew who might serve as a good test case :-)

Sorry for babbling so long off-topic,
