> questions; I hope I can find the rest in the documentation.  Is there
> anything important not on the VMS documentation CD (i.e. on other CDs or
> only in printed manuals)?
> 
> How does one physically connect the machines (cables, ports etc)?
> 
> What is the oldest VMS VAX version supported for clustering with VMS 7.1
> ALPHA?

You would have to check with DEC to see what is supported.  Having said
that, we have successfully clustered Alpha VMS 7.1 with VAX VMS 5.5-2. 
There are a few restrictions.  For example, MONITOR CLUSTER doesn't
work.  There might even be some patches available to fix that.
 
> Can the two VAX machines share a system disk, or does each machine have
> to have some (perhaps minimal) system disk of its own?

They can't directly share a system disk.  However, one could have a
local system disk which it serves over the Ethernet to the other.
 
> Can the VAX machines access the CD-ROM drive in my ALPHAstation?  What
> about the diskette drive (which is not SCSI)?

Yes, with qualifications.  The CDROM can be MSCP served once the systems
are up and running.  You can NOT use it to install VMS on the
VAXstations.  You can probably remove the CDROM from the ALPHAstation
and connect it to the VAXstation to do installations.

FWIW: I don't know if MSCP serving of CDROMs is officially supported. 
It seems to work, but sometimes it locks up and we have to reboot the
cluster to clear it.
 
> Is it possible/desirable to have an ethernet address (and hence direct
> accessibility over the net) for just one machine?

I'm not sure what you mean.  Each machine has it's Ethernet address
encoded in a chip on the board.  You may use the Ethernet address for
booting a machine, but you rarely use it for network communications. 
For DECnet you use the DECnet address.  For TCP/IP you use the IP
address.

Are you asking if you have to run DECnet on all machines?  If so, I
don't think so.  If you are asking about TCP/IP, I don't know enough to
tell you.

> Can I quickly change, unknown to the outside world, which machine has
> the one ethernet address (and name)?

Take a look at "cluster alias" in the DECnet manuals and see if that is
what you want?

> Which machine should be the main cluster machine?

There isn't a "main" cluster machine.  All nodes are members of the
cluster.  Depending on how you configure it, they may have different
roles, i.e., disk server, boot server, satellite, etc.

It sounds like you need to read through the clustering manuals and
figure out what you want to do.  There is a lot of flexibility in
configuring clusters and there isn't one "right" way to do it.

Good luck!

