> RSTS/E (at least V10.1, but I suspect this goes back forever) won't
> roll the date over correctly at end-of-year for more than one year
> after the boot date.  To test Y2K stuff, you need to reboot and set the
> date at boot time to 01-Jan-1999 or later.
> 
> I have a theory as to why this is the case, but haven't been able to
> ...
> 
> RSTS/E internal date format is an unsigned word whose value is
> (YYYY-1970)*1000+DOY, where YYYY is the year and DOY is the day
> of the year.  For example, 31-DEC-1997 is 27365, and 01-Jan-1998
> is 28001.
> 
> My theory is there is a word, computed at boot-time, containing the
> RSTS/E format date of the last day of the year (or maybe the last day+1,
> i.e. "32-DEC" or 27366 for systems booted in 1997), and at midnight on
> 31-DEC, when the clock service interrupt increments the date word, it
> compares the date to this word, and if they are the same, it resets the
> date to 01-JAN-YY+1.  This method would save a fair amount of code in the
> clock service to check every night at midnight if this is 31-DEC
> (including handling leap year).  The cost of this method is that end-of-
> year won't work right if the system is up for more than a year (the
> longest uptime I've ever seen on a RSTS/E system was about 9 months), or
> if you set the date to after 31-DEC of the boot year (or before 01-JAN of
> the boot year.)  Apparently the SET-TIME sys() call doesn't update this
> word.

John, you are as close as Damn It! is to swearing!
 
