Doug wrote:
> 
> brianh@bit3.com wrote:
> 
> > With today's technology, Linux
> >is a multi-hundred-thousand line project.
> 
> Yeah, but with 100 monkeys all typing in parallel, it
> didn't take too long to write....

Actually, the monkeys weren't typing in parallel, they
were using in parallel. The trick with Linux hasn't
been to get a huge base of coders WRITING in parallel,
but to get a huge base of users TESTING in parallel.

10,000 monkeys all installing and running in parallel can
catch a lot of bugs. When a fair fraction of those monkeys
are technically oriented and have the kernel source, most
of the bugs that get caught also get fixed.

See "The Cathedral and the Bazzar" by Eric Raymond for
further clarification.

<http://earthspace.net/~esr/writings/cathedral-bazaar>

- Jeff Dutky



