Bernd Paysan  <bernd.paysan@remove.muenchen.this.org.junk> wrote:
>brianh@bit3.com wrote:
>> Does it take advantage of advanaces in computer languages that Motif
>> does not/could not- specifically, object orientation?  Remember that
>> Motif is written in C.
>
>Yes, certainly. MINOS is written in an OO extension of Forth, which
>itself loads on standard Forth - it's only 600 lines, either.
>
>However, Motif is written based on Xt, which has some sort of "poor
>man's object oriented C" features. A properly done special purpose OO
>extension can make C programs nearly as compact as C++ programs. Not
>that I would say Xt and Motif are properly done ;-).

Motif is a wonderful example that you can do OO programming in a non-OO
language, but that there are reasons for using an OO language, IMHO.
And it was known decades ago (and is busily being rediscovered by my
generation) that the programming language you use greatly affects your
productivity- The author of "The Mythical Man Month" (which should, IMHO,
be required reading of all computer programmers) whose name escapes me
at the moment, claimed with proof that PL/1 programmers were 5 times
more efficient than assembly language programmers- not because they were
intrinsicly better programmers (they produced on average the same number
of lines of code per year), but because each line of PL/1 code was
worth five lines of assembler.

I'll also not argue that professional programmers use language tools 10-20+
years out of date.  There is a philosophy of "I'm so busy writting code
I don't have time to learn how to write code better"- a self-defeating
attitude if ever there was one.

However, I state bodly that there are still programs that take 30,000 lines
of code in OO-Forth (they just took 300,000 lines in C++ and 3,000,000 lines
in C).  There are even programs that take 300,000 lines.  My original
statement- that 30,000 line programs are intrinsicly different to write
than 3,000 line programs, and that there is another such difference
between 30,000 line programs and 300,000 line programs, remains.

The "next order of magnitude" (3,000,000 line programs) I have philospohical
problems with.  I beleive (with some justification) that we (humans) are
unable to design and build programs more complex than the complexity
a single human can deal with.  A single human being can understand a
300,000 line program (you need a talented human being and he/she needs
to work at it, but it can be done)- I don't think this is true at
3,000,000 lines.

>"Late answers are wrong answers!"

Wrong answers are also late answers...


I don't speak for Bit 3.
Brian





