Sean Burke  <Iburke@earthlink.net> wrote:
>
>Was the old style better?  Well, there's nothing like running 
>yesterday's software on today's machines - one reason the BSD
>system is so popular today. Perhaps our code will in turn be 
>described as "lean-n-mean" by those who follow us. But don't 
>bet on it. In the Mac world, the term "byte-squeaking pinheadery" 
>has been applied to some of the brilliant coding that enabled 
>the original MacOS to run in 128K.
>

Such brilliant coding has (and I have been lead to beleive) caused real
problems in maintainance.  The "brilliant hacks" which allowd you to 
shoehorn a GUI OS into 128K in 1984 are the "Backwards compatibility 
nightmares" of 1998.  This is one of the things I learned the hard way-
in my golden youth and college days, I never had to go back and maintain
software, and (at least in college) I (occasionally) got extra points
for unrequested feeping creaturism (and I never got docked for it).  By
making the code unnecessarily complex, however, I made the later code
maintainance a nightmare, and one of the reasons why having to maintain
my own code six months later caused an epiphany ("Unneeded complexity is
bad").

In the case of the Mac, I can't call it.  Was fitting it into 128K
absolutely required?  Would the Mac have died if it had debuted for $500
more with 256K?  I don't know.  There is some evidence that this might
actually have been the case- the NeXT debuted with system requirements
that weren't 'cheap' for another several years (68040 w/8M of memory).
This is one of the generally accepted reasons why it died.

>-SEan 
>
>(Think about it - OS, window system, and even space left to run 
> a simple word processor, in 128K! Boggles the mind, doesn't it? )

Yes, especially the people that claim this should be a requirement in the
era where most computers have more L2 cache than this.  Computing
power does change, and this changes the implicit requirements- limiting
strings to 32K on a system with only 128K of memory may make sense-
on a system with 128M of memory (and another 512M of swap), it's kinda
ridiculus.  Which is the greater crime- requiring the computing power
be available, or not taking advantage of the computing power that is
available?

I don't speak for Bit 3.
Brian




