Subject: [Q]: VMS port of Secure Shell (ssh)?
Date: 1998/02/25
Message-ID: <1998Feb25.154952.1@sldb4.slac.stanford.edu>#1/1
X-Deja-AN: 328671113
Organization: Stanford Linear Accelerator Center
Newsgroups: comp.os.vms,comp.sys.dec,vmsnet.networks.tcp-ip.multinet,vmsnet.sysmgt


        Has anyone ported, or attempted  to  port, Secure Shell (ssh) to
    VMS?  

        For those not familiar with  Secure Shell, it provides encrypted
    TCP/IP  connections using any of a number of methods (including DES,
    RSA, MD5, etc.) such that a connection from a home PC via the user's
    ISP to, e.g., SLAC systems not only  prevents  clear-text  usernames
    and  passwords  from  appearing  in  the  "dangerous  world  of  the
    Internet" out there, but _all_ transactions between the home desktop
    and the on-site ssh server are  (or  may  be) encrypted.  Here a few
    URLs giving more information:

            http://www.cs.hut.fi/ssh/                 
            http://www.uni-karlsruhe.de/~ig25/ssh-faq/
            http://www.datafellows.com/               
            http://www.Europe.DataFellows.com/        

        After dabbling  with  Kerberos  and  finding  too  many problems
    (incompatible  versions of Kerberos (K4 vs.  AFS/Kerberos), lack  of
    clients on wintel and Macintosh, etc.), it looks as if Secure  Shell
    may  cover  our  off-site  access  needs.   Secure  Shell  is freely
    available for  unix  and  linux  (we've  installed  it  on  our unix
    systems),  and  there are inexpensive Windows and Macintosh  clients
    available commercially (from Data Fellows,  see  URLs  above).   The
    product  works  at  a  level just above the network protocols and so
    doesn't require any modifications to _applications_, like Xwin-32 or
    eXodus, etc.  This looks like it's the way to go for us...

        There's been some interest  expressed  on the Multinet list over
    the  past  few  months,  but no comment (yet?)  from  Process  about
    whether they might support Secure Shell in Multinet (or TPCware  for
    that  matter).   While  I  have written a somewhat lengthy letter to
    Process requesting this support, we'd  like  not to have to wait for
    them (especially if they should decide _not_ to support it).  We are
    downloading  the unix source code and have an engineer just starting
    to take a look at it.

        If anyone has  attempted  the  port,  or  has _accomplished_ the
    port,  to VMS, we'd surely like to hear from you!  My presumption is
    that a port layered on Multinet using the UCX interface  would  also
    work  with  UCX.   I don't know TCP/IP programming (at all!), so I'm
    not  sure  whether  a  "layered"  approach  will  work,  or  whether
    something like this needs to be integrated into the Multinet Server,
    etc.  Thoughts from anyone?

        -Ken
