On what basis do you say that it "has to deliver better performance"?

While it would be embarrassing to Intel if Merced had lower performance than
another IA32 implementation, Intel would be more embarrassed if AMD or Cryix
et al, beat both Intel's IA32 and IA64.

It looks to me like Sanders (AMD) is gunning for the top of the IA32
performance heap with K7.

Remember that Intel is the prototypical "only the paranoid survive"
corporation.  Intel has been aggressively eating their young, and if they
have to they will certain consume an IA64 fetus in order to stay ahead of
AMD and Cryix.

So far, eating their young has been very satisfying.

Killing off the 486 one year earlier than a Harvard MBA would recommend
resulted in a large enough volume to pay for the vast increase in fab
capacity.  Then killing off Pentium just as it was reaching its stride,
returned even bigger volume increases.  And now Intel is trying very hard to
kill off Pentium MMX and the entire socket 7 world by the end of the year.
Note that they are willing to forgo profit margin in the process of killing
socket 7 since the Pentium II low end will almost certainly cost Intel
several dollars in margin between the cost of the Intel CPU and motherboard
for what Intel could have delivered a Pentium MMX solution for.




