Anil Thomas Maliyekkel wrote in message <6cco94$5jr$1@joe.rice.edu>...

>There was an article on Techweb recently about Compaq joining
>yet another SCO 64 bit UNIX alliance this past week.  Things
>don't look so clear for either Alpha or Digital UNIX.


Actually, the grim picture isn't about Alpha or Digital UNIX.  It just so
happened that I put this "UNIX camps" chart together this afternoon.

HP - HP/UX for PARISC, doing a port to IA64 - will be supporting two unix
implementation for ? years

Sun - Solaris for SPARC, IA32, doing a port to IA64 - will be supporting
three unix implementations forever
    NCR - NCR UNIX, for IA32, depending on Sun allowing them to make Solaris
for IA64 as robust

IBM - AIX for Power family, NOT doing a port to IA-64

SCO - UnixWare for IA-32, porting to IA-64 AND making it robust enough for
data center, supporting forever
    Compaq - selling UnixWare, investing in SCO port
    Tandem - NonStop UX on MIPS, NonStop for UnixWare on IA32, moving to
IA64, supporting forever
    ICL - selling UnixWare, investing in SCO port
   DG - DG/UX on IA32, investing in SCO port and contributing technology,
supporting DG/UX for ? years
   Unisys - UnixWare on IA32, investing in SCO port

DEC - Digital UNIX on Alpha, porting to IA-64 and enhancing both with
Sequent contributions, supporting both
   Sequent - Dynix on IA32, working with DEC and contributing technology on
move to IA64, supporting both
   HDS - working with DEC on mainframe class services for unix, unix
relationship not yet stated

This list vastly over simplifies the current situation.

[One thing is clear, Ken Olsen was right when he said unix compatibility is
snake oil, and it is clear why Windows has won the desktop and low end
server market.  The above helps explain why Oracle supports something over a
hundred unique ports of Oracle software; while a lot are on non-unix
platforms, a lot are on slightly varient unix platforms.   (Oracle must be
the leader in the amount of code times the number of ports - a truely
significant example of disciplined engineering).]

Digital has uncharacteristically pursued an alliance strategy, and this was
known to Compaq prior to their agreement to purchase DEC.  In fact, DEC has
entered into an agreement with Sequent prior to Compaq's negotiation, so
Compaq is obliged to honor the deal.

Sequent's Casey Powell was very clear on his desire for a bullet proof 64
bit unix for IA-64.  Sequent will be providing an environment that will run
apps written for Dynix on IA64 and he would clearly love to extend it to
UnixWare apps as well.  His view is that Sequent will win as long as the
apps are there because he will build the best IA64 hardware.

SCO is also clearly pursuing, and apparently succeeding at, an alliance
strategy, given that it has very well respected Compaq/Tandem in its camp.

HP failed at its attempt to form an alliance with SCO.

Sun failed to win Sequent in a year, while DEC succeeded in about a month.
I can't figure out why NCR is still in the Solaris camp given Solaris
Intel's  poor reputation compared to NCR's unix offerings.

So, my reading is that Compaq has a unix strategy if it fails to acquire
DEC.  And if it does complete the deal, it will be in a solid position to
unite the SCO and DEC unix efforts.  It could even do it by buying SCO.
since HP, Sun, and IBM would be forced to admit that the only way that SCO
could survive is by being bought out.  ;-)
Any other answer would mean that their flavor of unix faces stiff
competition from SCO since HP and Sun have said that DEC isn't a threat to
them.

Since it a general rule that a mature market can support only three major
players, with the third being weak, who out of the list (ordered by
corporate revenue) gets deleted?
    IBM - AIX
    Compaq/SCO/Tandem/DEC/Sequent/ICL/Unisys/DG/HDS - ????
    HP - HP/UX
    Sun/NCR - Solaris

Which of the remaining three becomes the weak sister?

Which of the above looks a lot like Microsoft's Windows strategy?

Which of the above leverage Intel's success plus have a hedge against
Intel's failure or domination?
Hint, which of the above looks most like IBM?

The computer analysts figure Sun is most at risk.  But they have Java.  HP
does have printers to fall back on when their unix can't sustain their
growth, but that's a pretty competitive market these days and HP is falling
behind.




