>I must say it's hard to fathom how VMS could be so good and NT
>could turn out to be such a dog, what with the same guy working
>on both of them...
LAN Times says that the best TCP benchmarks of Intel 4-way SMP systems are
those with Win NT.
I'm not a systems programmer but know from experience that NT systems:
    1. need to be tuned to achieve maximum performance. With a VMS system
you have an administrator person. With NT your are more likely to do
everything yourself. Important issue is that most people who use NT don't
know it well enough to tune it well.

    2. need to be programmed completely differently than DOS or UNIX. If you
use C library functions instead of such features as memory-mapped files,
async. io and async. networking, etc. you lose a lot of performance. So
there is a problem of application developers who don't know NT well enough
to program it correctly.
Furthemore API documentation is not always sufficient. I had to do *spying*
using system debugger to figure out details of some system calls.

 3. are hardware-sensitive. From my experience all cases of "blue screen of
death" are related to hardware compatibily problems. Once fully compatible
hardware is in place NT's rock-solid; NT and cheap hardware don't go along
well. You are talking about VAX/VMS which has very strict requirements to
hardware being more stable than NT.
But NT runs on hardware of 1000's of different vendors. Another example is
Solaris/PC. I heard that it dies during installation on some PCs. The point
here is that it's not fare to compare reliability of OS that runs on
proprietory hardware (developed by the same company) with OS that runs on
just about everything. And if you do compare take high-end NT hardware:
Compaq workstation without unusual hardware options for example.

