>On what basis do you say that it "has to deliver better performance"?
>
>While it would be embarrassing to Intel if Merced had lower performance
than
>another IA32 implementation, Intel would be more embarrassed if AMD or
Cryix
>et al, beat both Intel's IA32 and IA64.
>
>It looks to me like Sanders (AMD) is gunning for the top of the IA32
>performance heap with K7.
>
>Remember that Intel is the prototypical "only the paranoid survive"
>corporation.  Intel has been aggressively eating their young, and if they
>have to they will certain consume an IA64 fetus in order to stay ahead of
>AMD and Cryix.
>
>So far, eating their young has been very satisfying.
>
>Killing off the 486 one year earlier than a Harvard MBA would recommend
>resulted in a large enough volume to pay for the vast increase in fab
>capacity.  Then killing off Pentium just as it was reaching its stride,
>returned even bigger volume increases.  And now Intel is trying very hard
to
>kill off Pentium MMX and the entire socket 7 world by the end of the year.
>Note that they are willing to forgo profit margin in the process of killing
>socket 7 since the Pentium II low end will almost certainly cost Intel
>several dollars in margin between the cost of the Intel CPU and motherboard
>for what Intel could have delivered a Pentium MMX solution for.

It looks now like Willamette and Merced will be out in the same timeframe -
late 99. I would expect that they will be interchangeable in systems. BYTE
mag's guess was 28 SPECint95 for Willamette (IA32) and 50 SPECint95 for
Merced (IA64). Unless Merced is substantially more expensive than Willamette
(which doesn't bode well for its future) we can expect <28 SPECint95 for
Merced (IA32). A Pentium II style IA32 implementation would deliver >20 at
600 MHz. However a PII is the opposite of Merced in terms of scheduling with
Merced being rigid in-order, issue and execution and PII being dynamic
everything - IMHO bolting a PII core on the side of a IA64 processor seems
expensive on silicon but doable. OTOH using the Pentium approach with dual
in-order pipes fits better with the Merced rationale - with
IA32->IA64/internal translation. In this case the IA32 implementation looks
more like K5/K6 style. Note that the core would still benefit the larger
caches, branch prediction, clock speed etc. relative to Pentium.
So my guess is that Merced IA32 performance will be similar to a 600 MHz
Pentium MMX or roughly a 400-450 MHz Pentium II. About 18 SPECint95 for
Merced (IA32).

Of course I know nothing - so correct me.
--
