Bill Moyer <billm@cygnus.com> wrote:
}brianh@bit3.com writes:
}>I held a lot of the same opinions in my younger days that a lot of the
}>young hackers hold.  For example, comments are for dweebs who can't read
}>code.  And then I encountered the Real World (tm).  And had several
}>epiphanies.

}  I wish I'd run into the same experience .. as a young programmer, I
}got the importance of useful comments drilled into my head, and when I
}started working on commercial code I'd suddenly find more-senior engin-
}eers telling me to cut most of my comments.

     "In all things, moderation".

     ;
     ; In my ye olde days of mainframes, there was
     ; a big piece of code I worked on which had
     ; a tendency to massive comments blocks
     ; which would in great detail explain what
     ; the next few (or three or two or one!)
     ; lines of code were for even if it was
     ; not particularly disobvious.
     ;

     for (e = x; am < pl; ++e) {

     ;
     ; Personally, I found it hard to get 'enough'
     ; of the program in front of my eyes to grasp
     ; what was going on 'in the big picture'.
     ;

         am = p(l,e);

     ;
     ; Now, perhaps I am just wierd in this regard.
     ; And perhaps I'm just brainwashed by my early
     ; education which was that if a function is
     ; bigger than a page, split it up.
     ;

         pl = ea(s,e);

     ;
     ; Similarly, people who use small tab stops,
     ; and wide screens (or heaven forbid letting
     ; lines wrap all willy nilly) so they can have
     ; eleventy-dozen levels of nesting.  Argh!
     ;

         if (am)
         {
            ++pl;
         }
          else
         {
            --e;
         }

     ;
     ; Heaven help me for opening this can of worms,
     ; but I also have the same problem (in C) with the
     ; 'super-airy, each brace deserves it's own line style'.
     ;

         am ? ++pl : --e;

     ;
     ; Well, that's my 2c worth.
     ;

     }

     ;
     ; Like anyone cares.
     ;


