The original poster (the attribution seems to have disappeared) sounded
like he was saying that every 30,000 line program could be a 3,000 line
program if it was just written by a talented enough programmer.  Yes,
incompetance can lead to serious code bloat (turning 3,000 line programs
into 30,000 line programs), but there are 30,000 line programs which are
well written by talented people, and could not be turned into equivelent
3,000 line programs at the current state of the art.

Moving from assembly to C reduces the lines of code required for 
"equivelent" programs by at least a factor of 3- so the ~300,000 line 
Linux kernel in C would require ~1,000,000 lines (or more) of assembly- so 
advances in the state of the art reduce the number of lines of code
required for the same program.  However, the number and complexity of
features advance at the same time (if not before)- consider the complexity
difference going from a text-based word processor to a GUI WYSIWYG word 
processor.

