"mulp - cluster buster" <pettengill@mail.dec.com> writes:

>HP - HP/UX for PARISC, doing a port to IA64 - will be supporting two unix
>implementation for ? years


HP did a lot of the design on IA-64 (some say almost all of it, and that
Intel is mainly contributing the process and IA-32 compatibility stuff)
And it will run PA-RISC code.  That gives them a pretty nice advantage
over any other non IA-32 based vendors in terms of ease of moving their
installed base.  No one (who can say) knows for sure whether Merced
includes hardware support for PA-RISC or IA-32, but even if it won't
directly translate and execute the code, it is very likely there is at
least some support in there that will make this task easier.  Even if it
is all software or there is a major software component, having access to
the IA-64 instruction set a couple years ahead of everyone else is a huge
advantage to HP versus the other Unix vendors who plan ports to IA-64.

Remember, HP isn't just planning a port, they are planning a migration,
and intended that from the start (back when IA-64 was the PA-9000 that
HP was hiring all the old VLIW guys for)  So long as Merced is actually
faster than any of the PA-RISC chips available or close to available at
the time of its introduction (HP has the PA-8500 ready to introduce in a
few months, and the PA-8700 in development) this will work out fine for
them.  They only have to support HP-UX on both for a long time if Merced
can't keep up with PA-RISC, which is hopefully pretty unlikely or HP and
Intel wasted a lot of their best minds developing it!

-- 
