In article <34F1FF84.7CCB@hr.nl>, Martijn Haan <cwmhaan@hr.nl> writes:

  This question would probably be better suited to comp.os.vms, a
  newsgroup specifically targeted to the OpenVMS VAX and OpenVMS
  Alpha operating systems, and related discussions...

:Does anybody know what problems we can get with porting to an AXP?
:We have some information about alignment and hashinh-tables!
:Does anybody has written a document or done a project on this subject?

  There exists an entire manual on this topic in the OpenVMS Alpha
  documentation (full) set.  I would recommend starting there,

  For user-mode programs that rely on documented interfaces, it's
  usually little more than a recompilation and relink.  Porting of
  kernel-mode code and of code that is dependent on characteristics
  of the VAX calling standard -- such as code that traverses the
  call stack -- can be a more involved.  

  Be aware that some old application code can sometimes be replaced
  with documented OpenVMS calls that were added to OpenVMS after the
  original code was written, or that existed when the code was written,
  but that the programmer was unaware of or unwilling to use.  This
  willingness to replace application code with standard routines can
  sometimes eliminate whole modules and complex routines...

  Alignment of structures is normally handled by the compiler and
  is typically entirely transparent, and normally only becomes a
  factor when there are assumptions on the padding within the data
  structure -- such as when sharing the data structure between
  OpenVMS VAX and OpenVMS Alpha, for instance.

:Are there problems with the year 2000 on an AXP with VMS 7.1?

  Please see "VMS6.   Does OpenVMS have a problem with the year 2000?"
  in the OpenVMS Frequently Asked Questions (FAQ).  The OpenVMS FAQ is
  available at the URL:

    ftp://ftp.digital.com/pub/Digital/dec-faq/vms

  The above -- though I would still recommend getting and reading a
  copy of the FAQ for what should be obvious reasons -- will point
  you at the website URL:

    http://www.openvms.digital.com/openvms/products/year-2000/index.html

:Are there problems with using DMQ, Motif, Pascal, Dec-set etc..?

  The URL for the website for the current DIGITAL Year 2000 (Y2K)
  product information is:

    http://www.digital.com/year2000/

  The OpenVMS FAQ is an invaluable resource for answering many of the
  more common questions that folks would otherwise tend to ask here.
  Again and again...  Suggestions and updates to the FAQ are welcome,
  as well.  (I can say that since I'm not maintaining that FAQ.  :-)

