In article <34F18A1C.81428557@iisc.com>, Charles M Richmond <cmr@iisc.com> writes:

|> I have the OpenBSD 2.2 CDs and intend to install onto a 5000/25. I 
|> would like to see some discussion of the relative merits of NetBSD
|> 1.3.x vs OpenBSD 2.2.x (non religious of course (-: ).

The short answer is that both OpenBSD and NetBSD work well.

It's almost certainly impossible to say any more without causing
religious flamewars.  The OpenBSD/pamx codebase started with the same
pmax-specific code that NetBSD had at the time.  I don't know what has
happened to OpenBSD's pmax specific code since then: you should ask an
OpenBSD developer.

Since then there's been substantial improvement in performance and
reliability in the NetBSD code -- retuned Ethernet drivers and
improvements of 50% or more in the asc SCSI driver.  And also support
for the r4000 and r4400 models; and other work is in progress.

Any comparison of OpenBSD and NetBSD should include those improvements.

One of the things I keep hearing about OpenBSD is the care given to
security issues. Any comparsion should include that, too.

In the _specific_ case of pmaxes, the NetBSD developers include people
with extensive pre-NetBSD experience with the pmax hardware and
building OSes for it, including one person who worked on it at DEC.

*I* personally think that today, NetBSD/pmax 1.3.x supports more pmax
hardware, and supports it better, than does OpenBSD/pmax 2.2.x.

But I don't use OpenBSD, so dont take my word for it.



