Thor Lancelot Simon wrote:
> 
> In article <34F18A1C.81428557@iisc.com>,
> Charles M Richmond  <cmr@iisc.com> wrote:
> >Jonathan Stone wrote:
> >>
> >> In article <6cr4rt$aap@panix2.panix.com>, tls@panix.com (Thor Lancelot Simon) writes:
> >> |> In article <6cq6j4$sh4$2@nntp.Stanford.EDU>,
> >> |> Jonathan Stone <jonathan@DSG.Stanford.EDU> wrote:
> >> |> >In article <34ef6e01.0@news.triax.com>, rmohr@fta.com (Rob Mohr) writes:
> >> |> >
> >> |> >> I am considering installing NetBSD 1.3 on my DECstation 3100.  I was
> >> |> >> wondering if anyone had any experience with running NetBSD on the
> >> |> >> DECstation and could point out any of the pros and cons relating to
> >> |> >> it.  I am currently running Ultrix 4.4 and have had some difficulty
> >> |> >> compiling some programs such as perl 5 and PPP and hope that I will
> >> |> >> have better luck with a "newer" OS.
> >
> >I had no problem compiling & running PPP on Ultrix 4.4 with the
> >serial port speed set to 19200.
> 
> Your definition of "no problem" may be different than many people's.  The box
> just basically *will* drop data at "19200" bps.  Depending whether you have
> extremely copious logging turned on, you may not notice this with PPP running
> except as a decrease in PPP throughput -- a substantial one -- when the
> machine gets busy.
> 
> >> |> >
> >> |> >I'm not a good person to speak about the `cons' of running NetBSD.
> >> |> >The big `con' is that the 3100 hardware is just not suitable for
> >> |> >running PPP. The serial hardware is a single-chip DZ-11 clone.
> >> |> >
> >> |> >That means the serial ports do not do full modem control and will not
> >> |> >run at above 9600 baud. (I think that's right for the 3100; they might
> >> |> >do 19200, but not reliably.)
> >
> >Again, I was able to run PPP at 19200 without problem.
> 
> Well, you were able to run PPP at "19200" "without problem".  I just booted
> my Ultrix disk on my 2100 again to check -- as of the Ultrix 4.0 Field Test
> which is on that disk (which dates back many years to the summer I worked at
> DEC) if you told Ultrix "19200" it would claim to be speaking at 19200,
> but on a 2100/3100 would actually speak out the serial port at 9600.  This is
> probably a Good Thing, since many/most modern devices aren't tolerant enough
> to handle the DZ11's 19800 approximation of 19200bps.
> 
> Of course with a modern modem with automatic DTE rate, Ultrix would allege to
> you that you were running at 19200, the modem would autobaud to 9600, and
> you might never notice.  I certainly didn't for years. :-)

The throughput at the time was pretty obviously much higher than
9600 baud. Comparing a 4.0 FT Ultrix to 4.4 is like comparing
an X10 implementation to X11R5. 


> >I have the OpenBSD 2.2 CDs and intend to install onto a 5000/25. I
> >would like to see some discussion of the relative merits of NetBSD
> >1.3.x vs OpenBSD 2.2.x (non religious of course (-: ).
> 
> Hopeless; it will get religious.  What Jonathan's already posted on the
> subject is about all that one can say without getting bogged down in flamage.
> 
> Note that all of the developers responding to questions about these issues
> are working on NetBSD, and that the NetBSD developers number several people
> with fairly extensive experience with the pmax hardware, including one who
> worked on it at DEC.  Perhaps this will help you make a decision.
> 

Well I also worked on pmax drivers (SCSI) and later on a wide variety
of Digital Unix OS code while contracting at both DEC and at OSF, not 
to mention the Thinking Machines kernel (scheduling), Solaris (kernel
support), etc, etc.  I really would like to see some technical 
discussion of the differences. I wish that I had the time to really
go digging on my own, but nowadays my time is either paid for or 
belongs to my business or to my wife (-:

Charlie

