	Careful there laddy.  I assume that "lame embedded data base in
	the file system" you are referring to refers to RMS.  RMS is a huge
	win if you know how to use it.  But more importantly, that embedded
	database you are referring to allows you to order writes... with
	writes ordered you can lock/unlock things in the OS...  yes high lock 
	traffic in a cluster but now move the cluster inside a box and you get 
	I/O scaling that you can't do with other OSes.  What is it called?
	Galaxy.  You will see I/O scaling at the end of this year that will
	not be matched by other OSes with corresponding "astronomical"
	TPC numbers.  VMS is very well engineered like many UNIXes.  VMS takes
	a different approach at the filesystem level and it is a far superior
	approach.  It just will take to the end of this year to prove it.

