Dear Klaus,

I work here at Appian Graphics Technical Support and I'd like to answer your
question.

First of all the graphics card is an Appian Technologies Rendition display
card.  Dec used it in these 'Pizza Box' systems back in '91-'92.  Appian
Technologies discontinued this product line about '93 or so.

Appian Technologies filed for bankruptcy in 1995 and produced no Windows
drivers for this card.  The Rendition II uses a Texas Instruments TMS 34020
accelerator chip and there are no Win95/WinNT or Win3.1 drivers that support
this chip as far as I am aware.

Second, the monitors.  The monitors are FIXED FREQUENCY monitors--you need a
display card that can use the same synch. rate as the monitor in order for it
to work.  DEC had these babies customized for use with the Rendition.

The info I have (literally the last remaining Dec 422 manual at Appian--what
can I say, I'm a packrat) says that the VR320-DA and VR319-DA use a
Horizontal Freq of 77 KHz and a Vertical Freq of 72 Hz, ONLY.

Now, the Manual for the DEC/Rendition card is 42 pages.  I don't have a
scanner or such, but you could e-mail your fax number to me and I could zap
it over to you.
