bgInc. -- Choice is overrated wrote:
> You both forgot another HUGE mistake - wasting all that time and effort on
> OSI instead of TCP/IP. VMS could have had RMS transparent tcpip file access
> many years ago - it would have ruled the internet.

Easy to say in hindsight. But back then, the internet was still a
"hacker/scientist" university network. And governments, especially in europe
were mandating computer manufacturers to provide OSI otherwise they wouldn't
buy from them. Digital had no choice. 

The problem is that OSI cost DEC a lot of TIME and by the time it was
delivered, the TCP/IP was starting to become an acceptable solution to those
who had previously mandated OSI only solutions.

So, I do not hold the OSI decision against Digital.  It did, however, take a
long time to react to TCP/IP before it made it "standard" on VMS once it
became obvious that TCP/IP had won. The fact that companies such as Multinet,
Wollongong etc thrived for many years is an indication of how long DEC took to react.



