In article <6cpgna$oqo@panix2.panix.com>, tls@panix.com (Thor Lancelot Simon) writes:
> In article <1998Feb22.084722.1@eisner>,
> Larry Kilgallen <Kilgallen@eisner.decus.org.nospam> wrote:

>>"Dumbing down" VMS to typical TCP/IP standards is not a solution.
>>(Typically, any user can open a TCP/IP port for access from other
>>nodes, with no privilege or access control required.)
> 
> This is a significant misunderstanding of the way the security of typical
> TCP/IP applications works.  Not to mention that it implies a significant
> overstatement of the actual level of security of most DECnet applications...

Authentication (by the initiator) or privilege (by the target) is required
for any DECnet application.  If you are saying that some sites are careless
with those, I cannot disagree that those exist.  To avoid DECnet security,
however, you must actively discard it.

With TCP/IP, however there is no such general requirement (other than for
the well-known ports, and certain layered protocols such as Telnet). Lack
of security is the default.

"Dumbing down" VMS to typical TCP/IP standards is not a solution.

Larry Kilgallen



