In article <34ef6e01.0@news.triax.com>, rmohr@fta.com (Rob Mohr) writes:

> I am considering installing NetBSD 1.3 on my DECstation 3100.  I was
> wondering if anyone had any experience with running NetBSD on the
> DECstation and could point out any of the pros and cons relating to
> it.  I am currently running Ultrix 4.4 and have had some difficulty
> compiling some programs such as perl 5 and PPP and hope that I will
> have better luck with a "newer" OS.

I'm not a good person to speak about the `cons' of running NetBSD.
The big `con' is that the 3100 hardware is just not suitable for
running PPP. The serial hardware is a single-chip DZ-11 clone.  

That means the serial ports do not do full modem control and will not
run at above 9600 baud. (I think that's right for the 3100; they might
do 19200, but not reliably.)

NetBSD 1.3 is released and runs well on 3100s; 1.3.1 should be out in
about a week. Installation is pretty painless if you have a second
system to dd a diskimage onto disk, or to support installing via
diskless boot.  One big advantage is that ELF shared libraries means
you need much less disk space than Ultrix.

PPP comes with the OS (but see above) and there is precompiled perl5
binary package.


>If freeBSD is available for the DECstation I would consider that
> also.  Any advice would be appreciated.  Thanks in advance,

FreeBSD is not available. You might wish to consider OpenBSD, but
(AFAIK) their DECstation-specific code is based on an old version of
the NetBSD code and is missing many significant updates.

|> Rob Mohr



