> >
> > > DEC has made more than three big mistakes, but here's the SKD take on
> > > the Three Big Ones:

.... mistakes removed .... (if it were that easy for DEC! :-) )
 
> > > 1992-8: Failing to market Alpha aggressively.
> >
> > Brilliant un-marketing of anything DEC is more like it I think.
> > Try to buy a tulip PCI card at retail if you don't believe this.
> >
> > ~Paul
> 
> Which only goes to show that: Digital made more than three mistakes!

And probably Digital continues to do so....

Digital seemed to have lots of problems in the late 80s but they
still managed to be the world's #2 computer company. In the early
90s it looked like they decided their core business was hardware
to run commodity software. This was interesting given that their
successful sales pitch until then had been one operating system
(software) for everyone! They probably assumed that the Intel CISC
architecture was reaching the end of its life and they wanted to
be in a position to 'be' the replacement. (Didn't Intel surprise
everyone - now we have talk of 500Mhz systems by the end of the
year! And to think Digital once had the fastest CISC... :-) )

The strategy was to focus on Unix and NT despite all of the $'s
rolling in from VMS. However Unix sales were complicated by Digital
switching architectures (how are we going to make Ultrix systems
Y2K compliant??), and a view that other vendors had been in the
market place longer and were more reliable (existing VMS customers
were particularly wary!). NT sales really depended on Digital 
being able to offer a high end to the market - but with improvements
from Intel this has not really panned out. In the meantime most
of the existing VMS customers felt like they had no vendor support
at all (talk about killing the golden goose!)

One thing I would like to know is what position Digital have slipped
to now? I guess with the takeover by Compaq they are back up to #2?

With the takeover by Compaq it is unclear what will happen with
Unix support. It is unlikely that Compaq will drop any products
in the short term but they have other Unix offerings and may
eventually look to consolidate. One possibility would be to
concentrate on offerings for Merced?

Support of NT systems is also very interesting. Unless Digital
are able to push sales through services, clustering, applications
or other special offerings, they are really just continuing to
provide marketing support for Microsoft! (Not that Microsoft
really need this!) As cheap fast NT systems are available from
nearly every corner store it is getting harder to find an angle!

VMS is also at the cross roads. With no new versions for some
time and almost no effort to encourage existing customers, its
future is very uncertain. (However it has survived until now
despite what appears to be a very determined effort to kill it
off.) It will depend on what Compaq do (not say!) in the very
near future...

(It is probably worth mentioning that IBM have continued supporting
AS400 systems and have been significantly increasing sales in that
area! - Not bad when AS400s and VAXes were generally seen to have
the same sort of future a couple of years ago!)


My $0.02 worth

Paul Nankervis

These are personal opinions only!



