>I'm not a good person to speak about the `cons' of running NetBSD.
>The big `con' is that the 3100 hardware is just not suitable for
>running PPP. The serial hardware is a single-chip DZ-11 clone.  
>
>That means the serial ports do not do full modem control and will not
>run at above 9600 baud. (I think that's right for the 3100; they might
>do 19200, but not reliably.)

The chip can do 19800.  That's 19*8*00, not 19200.  It may or may not be close
enough to speak to various devices which want to talk at 19200.

Digital's driver wouldn't make the thing go at 19800 ("19200" to the
software) on a 3100.  I think it would on a 5000/200.  But of course the chip
totally sucks, so it's not very reliable even at 9600 even on a 5000.  And
there's no real hardware flow control on a 3100, you might get it on a
5000, IIRC.

Real DZ-11s did 19800, too, BTW.  So if you want to talk to one of those on
the other end, you're in good shape.  It probably can't send fast enough to
overflow you anyway... (to begin with, it's probably on a VAX!)

