Tony Griffiths (tonyg@OntheNet.com.au) wrote:

: The VMS development team was one of the most disciplined I have ever
: seem.  Coding was left strickly to the professionals.  'Raw' recruits
: (ie. college grads) DID NOT get their sticky little fingers on the code
: pool.  Their first job was bug fixing under the guide of an experienced
: mentor.  Only when they had proved themselves did they get to work on
: new development.

	From what I recall as a _user_, those raw recruits must have
done most of their "bug fixing" on the TTY driver and VMS MAIL. It was
amazing how those two subsystems got worse and more byzantine from
release to release. It really was a theory at our shop (high-point, 13
VMS Vaxen) that work on those two was either a punishment or a "trial
by fire" for newbies. An example for those blissfully unfamiliar with
the system: the terminal handler would often reject an attempt to
"restore" terminal characteristics that it had returned itself. Our
callback program would "reverse engineer" the _actual_ (as opposed
to the documented) meaning of the various bits at each boot, so
we didn't have to remember to do it each release.

: All external evidence is that this is NOT how M$ work today...

	AFAIK, this is not how _any_ successful (in the _business_
sense of the word) software house works. Two quotes from ex-bosses
of mine speak worlds:

1) "I've always felt that the perfect is the enemy of the adequate"
   (Sam Huey, V.P. Engineering, Digital Telephone Systems)

2) "If the job was easy, we'd get somebody cheaper than you to do it"
   (Steve Calfee, Time Warner Interactive)

	Steve was partially kidding, but I feel he summed up the view
of _his_ superiors pretty well. Sam was dead serious, but seems to have
missed the obvious correlary, and the inevitable slide to "The truly
adequate is the enemy of 'good enough to ship before we hop jobs'"

					Mike
| albaugh@agames.com, speaking only for myself



