In article <8605185405B336B5.AD19EE0847EEB909.9A2C339E4C6C7BC8@library-proxy.airnews.net>, "Bob Young" <rcyoung@groupz.net> writes:

>When monitor shows this value to be > 1.0, what specific options are
>available for correcting it at the system level??

I'm tempted to ask if you could somehow be less specific about whatever
problem you think you're having, but that would waste everybody's time.

Instead, please offer a clue what the configuration is and what
operating system is involved.  While I'm inclinced to think this
is an OpenVMS system, some versions of my Monitor for Digital
UNIX do display the queue depth.

If the underlying disk subsystem supports command queuing (many
SCSI disks do and all DSA disks do), a little queue depth over
1 may not be a bad thing.  Multiple commands will be sent to
the disk and it will sort them for (hopefully) a better overall
response time.  If the queue gets too deep, then the disk is
probably saturated and either reducing the load to the specific
disk or spreading the load out of more disks may be the only
solutions.

--
Alan Rollow				alan@nabeth.cxo.dec.com




