In article <wMqdig0nxFQH@malvm1.mala.bc.ca>, 
    		dunnett@mala.bc.ca (Malcolm Dunnett) writes:
> 
>   I have a Storageworks Raid array 310 ( HSZ-20 ) system.
> 
>   The system comes with the ability to use writeback cacheing. When
> this is enabled for a unit ( a partition of a raid 3/5 set ) a warning is
> printed on the console about using this feature with raid sets, and to
> see the manual for further information. I've looked through the manual
> and can't find any details about the pitfalls of doing this.

        What is the specific wanring  message?  From my experience (with
    HSJ's),  I'd  expect  a warning if you  _didn't_  enable  write-back
    caching.

>   What are your thoughts about using writeback cacheing in this
> setup. From a performance viewpoint the benefits are tremendous, but
> I'm not clear on how much danger the option presents - eg if there is
> a failure ( power or controller ) will only the files currently being
> written be affected or will the whole partition or raidset be damaged?

        Having  read   the   documentation   for   our  HSJ-50's  fairly
    thoroughly,  but  unfortunately not recently, I seem to recall  that
    write-back cache is turned on by _default_ for mirror sets and  RAID
    sets.   This  is not only a performance issue, it's a data-integrity
    issue given that  you  want  writes  to  complete  to  _all_ dataset
    members  in the event of a power failure, etc.  You must, of course,
    have battery backup (is that _optional_ on the HSZ-20?).   Once  the
    controllers   reboot/power-up/whatever,   any  previously  unwritten
    cached data (in the  battery-backed  writeback  cache) is written to
    the target devices (unless explicitly overridden).

        The only warning I recall is  that  the  HSx's will _not_ make a
    mirror  set  or  RAID set available (serve it)  after  a  controller
    reboot _if_ the battery(s) indicates low power or low charge.   Once
    the  battery(s)  are  sufficiently  charged,  the  datasets  will be
    served.  Perhaps this is the warning you saw?

        -Ken
