Content-Type: text/plain; charset=us-ascii
Organization: AT&T WorldNet Services
Mime-Version: 1.0
Newsgroups: comp.sys.dec


Modifying your paper tray may be the answer.  Requires a small paperclip and a
piece of tape.

1. Remove paper tray from printer.

2. Remove paper from tray

3. Note 2 black bumps on the bottom.  They support the leading edge of the
paper stack as it feeds into the printer.

4. Note that between these 2 black bumps, in the middle of the page, there is
an area of the tray where a 3rd black bump could be, but isn't.

5. Tape a small paperclip (or some other material that approximates the
thickness of the black bumps) to the area.  This raises the center of the
stack of paper, at the leading edge, to make it the height of the rest of the
leading edge of the stack.  This allows the paper to be "grabbed" by the
center pickup roller more reliably.



