In article <34eebee6.0@rzunews1.unizh.ch> ps@ocisgi7.unizh.ch (Serge Pachkovsky) writes:
>Gints Klimanis (gints@sgi.com) wrote:
>: I don't know exactly when this work started.  However, it's clear that
>: college students of the 90's and today have an attribute missing from
>: college graduates of earlier years:  an additional decade of
>: exposure to computers.  Although one can question the value of that
>: experience, that additional time on personal computers should not
>: be ignored. 
>
>It shouldn't be overestimated, either. Difficult access to slow and
>expensive computers encourages careful analysis of your algorithms,
>as well as design of comprehensive test sets for your particular
>implementation. It also makes advantages of looking for the right 
>solution, rather than the very first brute force hack which springs 
>to mind, quite obvious.  Actually, the mere fact of slowing down the
>bug-correction-rerun-bug cycle frequently results in *fewer* bugs 
>and *faster* debugging - by virtue of giving the programmer time 
>to think about the *reasons* for that particular bug rather than
>about its outward manifestation. In short, the mainframe packet
>access model promotes *thinking* and *discipline*. 

  The original poster was referring to the experience of highschool
graduates in the 80's.  This means 1MHz 8080's or 6502's with 16K,
32K, or (for the lucky) 64K of RAM and no hard disks.  Home computer 
programmers of this era had to analyze/choose their algorithms care-
fully, with a careful eye towards various bottlenecks, just so that
their ADM3's would update at a decent rate.  There was no brute force 
available, just slow signals trudging reluctantly down an S100 bus.

  "Large" compile tasks (that could not fit entirely in 32K RAM) 
had to swap off slow 8" floppy disks.  This could take hours, and 
had the same sort of effects on debugging technique as the main-
frame batch model.

  IMO, programmers from this era tend to be the best programmers
in the world.  They learned to program on hardware several orders
of magnitude less powerful than what is used today, but were young
and still learning when the industry and the technology started 
changing into its modern form.  Most older programmers' brains seem 
to have inculcated during this transition period. 

>Mind you, I am not trying to say that *any* kind of PC programming 
>experience is useless in this respect - writing a kernel driver for
>Linux would be a great boon. However, there mere fact of exposure
>to *some* PC programming is not necessarily beneficial.

  :-) Torvalds didn't start writing Linux until 1991.  Think CP/M.

  -- Bill Moyer

