Zalman Stern wrote:
> 
> Andy Glew (glew@cs.wisc.edu) wrote:
> : Actually, I think Allen's point was somewhat Intel-favourable
> : --- it makes sense to have a lot of people working on a chip  if,
> : thereby, the chip is made significantly smaller.
> 
> Or sooner.
> Or less buggy.
> 
> : So long as more people => smaller chips ...
> 
> So long as more people => sooner chips ...
> So long as more people => less buggy chips ...

But with more people, you often have the problem that the left hand
doesn't know what the right hand does - and coordination is very
difficult. Last month someone of the spec team here said (after
revealing informations that had been on his desk for months) "Oh, I seem
to have forgotten to communicate on this one". Ok, Siemens HL is not one
of the major players...

My experience: More people => bloated design => later+slower+larger+more
bugs. Well, more "features", though. Decentralized features - things you
have in one place are - differently - available at another place, too.
There's a typical company culture of "teamwork", which certainly
prevents competition, but worse, prevents critics (or reaction to
critics - nobody wants to loose their face). When asked about a rapid
prototyping development approach, someone said "we don't have the time
to do things twice" - but they "have" the time to complete brain-damaged
designs.

If you have more people at the end of the pipe (in synthesis/hand layout
and such like), yes, you get better chips. However, a clean, lean design
in the beginning saves much more time, space, bugs, etc. than any effort
at the end.

