In article <Pine.A41.3.96.980217235651.47054B-100000@asterix.uni-muenster.de>, "Marcus Daniel" <danielm@uni-muenster.de> writes:
> Hi folks :-)
> 
> I recently got hold of a VAXstation 3100 M76, sadly it came without any
> documentation.
> 
> Since NetBSD doesn't support neither graphics nor console on the 3100, I
> thought of giving the monitor to a friend and use a terminal as console
> (netbsd doesn't boot yet, I still have to tinker with the console).
> 
> I read somwhere that I just have to flip the little recessed switch on the
> back. When I do it the monitor doesn't show a picture, but the terminal
> program on my linux box (VT102 emulation) doesn'T show anything.

To the left of the 8 led status window (on the extreme right looking at the
back of the box) is a small switch in a recess.  It must be in the "up" pos-
ition to ise the terminal as the console.

>                               <-
> I used the serial port marked -> for the connection, I veryfied the
> connection cable with the DTE command on the console with monitor still
> attached and the cable connected to the serial printer port, both machines
> exchange keystrokes, but nothing useful happens.

Well then, you're using the wrong port!  You want to use the port which has 
what looks to be a small printer icon above it for the console port.  Set 
the terminal or terminal emulator to 9600 baud.  With the terminal on, power
up the VS3100.  You should see self-test codes on the screen if the VS3100
is functional and the communicating to the terminal.

> THe cable I use has a " kind of telephone jack" to RS232 male connector and
> I put a null-modem connector on it before attaching it to the linux box
> (without the null modem swithc nothing happens).
> 
> I use XON/XOFF 9600 8N1 and also tried 7N1.
        ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^

This should work when you plug into the correct "console" port!  You may or
may NOT need the null modem connector.

