In article <01bd3c66$7db6baa0$6214a6c3@turlington.unidec.co.uk>,
David Turlington <turlington@unidec.co.uk> wrote:
>We would like to hear from anyone who is running PDP 11/93-94 systems
>(Dec CPU's only) particulary in real time environments as the Y2K problem
>exists only if system is powerfailed after 1st-Mar-2000 it resumes with
>date 1st-Jan-1900 (also a day of week problem exists)

This is a problem with the console firmware on the 11/93-94's.  The
day of week problem - as far as I know - is purely cosmetic, as no operating
system actually uses the day of week register on the time-of-year chip.
But as you note, the rollover problem associated with 29-Feb-2000 to
1-Mar-2000 is well known (even mentioned on comp.risks a couple of
weeks ago!).  I've offered to make a patch to the console firmware
available, but the folks at both Mentec and Digital say it is *not*
possible for this to be distributed due to strict non-disclosure
agreements with ROI.

Certainly, one workaround is to not use the 11/9x on-board-TOY but instead
use a separate board, such as one of the CODAR's, that won't get hit
by the console firmware during reboot from powerfail.  It was a month or so
ago that I posted patches to the routines supplied by Codar for reading
and writing the TOY clock, and I've tested these patches under RT-11
5.7 and verified that they work.  They should also work under the latest
RSX-11M+, but I haven't actually tested this.

