In article <8605185405B336B5.AD19EE0847EEB909.9A2C339E4C6C7BC8@library-proxy.airnews.net>, "Bob Young" <rcyoung@groupz.net> writes:
:When monitor shows this value to be > 1.0, what specific options are
:available for correcting it at the system level??

  I'll assume this is an OpenVMS (comp.os.vms) question.

	--

  Specifically?  Options include reducing the I/O load on the disk
  spindle(s) by reducing the overall I/O load or by spreading the
  existing load over additional disk spindles or by going to larger
  I/Os, or by replacing the existing disk spindle(s) with faster
  disk spindles. 

  Reducing the I/O load can involve increasing the caches and increasing
  the RMS buffering and using global buffers.  Spreading the load can
  involve moving files around, and products such as host-based disk
  striping.  Moving to faster spindles involves faster disks and faster
  controllers, and moving to RAID controllers.

  These are (obviously) not mutually exclusive.

  Information on tuning applications is included in the _OpenVMS System
  Manager's Manual: Tuning and Complex Systems_, as well as application
  information in the RMS-related manuals such as the _Guide to OpenVMS
  File Applications_.

	--

  When posting, the operating system version, platform, and -- in cases
  such as this -- storage configuration -- are often of interest and
  should be considered for inclusion.  We can then provide more specific
  hardware and software suggestions.

