:I recently got hold of a VAXstation 3100 M76, sadly it came without any
:documentation.
..
:I read somwhere that I just have to flip the little recessed switch on the
:back. When I do it the monitor doesn't show a picture, but the terminal
:program on my linux box (VT102 emulation) doesn'T show anything.

  The switch is usually labeled "S3".  Setting S3 "Down" selects the
  workstation graphics display as the console, while "Up" selects the
  MMJ serial line with the printer icon as the console port.  See
  the OpenVMS FAQ for information on the MMJ pinout and adapters.
  Based on your comments, you appear to be using the communications
  port, not (as you should) the printer port...

  I'd expect to see the self-test text on the console (with S3 down)
  or on the serial terminal (with S3 up).

  Use <break> -- the <break> signal is a deliberately-induced serial
  line framing error -- to get the attention of the system when using
  the serial console, and halt the system.

:                              <-
:I used the serial port marked -> for the connection, I veryfied the
:connection cable with the DTE command on the console with monitor still
:attached and the cable connected to the serial printer port, both machines
:exchange keystrokes, but nothing useful happens.
:
:THe cable I use has a " kind of telephone jack" to RS232 male connector and
:I put a null-modem connector on it before attaching it to the linux box
:(without the null modem swithc nothing happens).
:
:I use XON/XOFF 9600 8N1 and also tried 7N1.

  "Kind of telephone jack" would appear to be the Modified Modular
  Jack (MMJ), which is a standard telephone jack -- there are three,
  of which the one with the locking tab centered is most familiar.

  9600 baud is the standard (and only) speed for the console serial
  line on this system.

:I wish I had some manuals to read ...

  EK-VSM30-OM was the VAXstation 3100 38 Owners Manual -- this and other
  documentation of this vintage is no longer readily available, however.
  (And no, you can't have my carefully hoarded copy of this manual. :-)

:Maybe you can help ... (Please also reply by email)

  Ask here, get an answer here.

