DevinRage (devinrage@aol.com) wrote:
: Does anyone know where I can get technical info on these terminals?   
: I've tried searching the DEC website to no avail.  Specifically I need 
: schematics on the pin-outs on the comm ports.  I have the OEM cable
: (with the proprietary RJ11 type connector) and would like to splice it
: on to a standard DB9 serial connector.  I'm sure I could go out and get
: an adapter from DEC but I have the tools and materials to make this in
: house.  Any help would be appreciated.  Please reply by email.
   
: TIA

I made one of these cables for a friend a couple months ago.  This is what
you'll need...

6-conductor modular cable
MMJ plugs (10 cents a piece or so)
MMJ crimper (This costs about $35 and no, you can't carve up a cheap
  plastic Radio Shack phone plug crimper.  I tried that already and had
  a carpet full of plastic shreds, a broken crimper, and an ill-fitting
  plug to show for it..)
   
At the shop I got this stuff from, I found what looks like a D-sub hood
with an MMJ jack where the cable should go in.  I wired it up so I could
put in on the DTE female 25-pin jack on the wacky HP terminal and put MMJ
cable from there to the VAX.  If you can get one of these things, you
really should use it instead of chopping up an MMJ cable.  It's a lot
neater. 

These are the pinouts.
  
DECconnect DEC-423 MMJ pinout:

   1 Data Terminal Ready (DTR)
   2 Transmit
   3 Transmit Ground
   4 Receive Ground
   5 Receive
   6 Data Set Ready (DSR)

  
  Pin assignments
  DB-25

  2 Transmit Data
  3 Receive Data
  4 Request to send
  5 Clear to send
  6 Data Set Ready
  7 Signal Ground
  8 Data Carrier Detect
  20 Data Terminal Ready
  22 Ring Indicator

  Pin Assignments
  DB-9
  1 Data Carrier Detect
  2 Receive Data 
  3 Transmit Data
  4 Data Terminal Ready
  5 Signal Ground
  6 Data Set Ready
  7 Request to Send
  8 Clear to Send
  9 Ring Indicator IMP.

  The cable build went something like this:
    
  MMJ - D25
  1 - 20
  2 - 2
  3 - 7
  4 - 7
  5 - 3
  6 - 6
   
You might have to reverse the transmit and receive pins.  I forget exactly
how they went.  You won't damage the machinery if they're wrong, however. 

I wonder if there's a FAQ maintainer for comp.sys.dec.  At least this
information should replace the barely useful information in the VMS faq.

--
David Griffith
dgriffi@ultrix6.cs.csubak.edu




