Hi,
   DECnet for Macintosh absolutely requires either motherboard
(built-in) Ethernet or a Nubus card.  The driver uses the old Mac slot manager.
It can't even be fooled into running on a PCI or PCMCIA or Comm. Slot interface.

This will not change because no further updates are planned.  The product
retires (and support stops entirely) in July.

Product Mangement queries can go to Jeff Babington, babington@mail.dec.com.
Technical queries can go to Eirikur Hallgrimsson, eh@jamin.enet.dec.com


Something that might work for you would be DECnet over AppleTalk, which is a
line/port option in NCP.  You need to have something like a FASTPATH
available to unwrap the packets, though.

Use TCP/IP if you can.  There are good FTP servers (FTPd, for one, now
called Netpresenz, I think), and PATHWORKS 1.3 provides and FTP client and
a Telnet tool for use with MacTerminal or any Comm. toolbox terminal
emulator.

I don't know of a DECnet solution that works on systems that don't have a
builtin adaptor.  The PATHWORKS DECnet is a version of the Thursby Software
Systems TSSnet product that was built by TSS for Digital.  My understanding
is that they are not planning to update TSSnet, either.  They are at
www.Thursby.com.


Regards,

Eirikur




