Andy Glew wrote:
> 
> > > What you should real do is compare the size of the design teams to
> > > determine development costs. I suspect you'd find the comparison
> > > reversed from the number of transistor comparison.
> > >
> > ... and it may be a meaningless metric:  perhaps Intel has spent an
> > inordinate amount of engineering resources maintaining binary
> > compatibility with 15-yo code and then performed engineering heroics to
> > squeeze respectable performance from the resultant architecture.  But
> > you can't argue with their balance sheet and bottom line.
> 
> Actually, I think Allen's point was somewhat Intel-favourable
> --- it makes sense to have a lot of people working on a chip  if,
> thereby, the chip is made significantly smaller.

   That is true only if 1) you are selling the chips as the end product and
   2) you expect to sell an awful lot of them.  Of course both of these are
   true for nearly all Intel's x86 products.

   The counter example to illustrate my point is the Sun MicroSPARC-I.  This
   bloated pig of a chip was built with a lot of help from automated tools and
   took 225 mm2 for 800,000 transistors in a 0.8 um, 2M CMOS process (compared
   to the handcrafted orginal 486 which crammed 1.2 million transistors into
   165 mm2 in a 1.0 um, 2M CMOS process.  It made sense for Sun because the
   microSPARC integrated many system level functions into the CPU and permitted
   a very low parts count workstation to be built cheaply and with a short time
   to market.

> 
> So long as more people => smaller chips ...

   Maybe "more effort" is more accurate than "more people" in terms of getting
   smaller chips.

All opinions strictly my own.

--
