In article <gurman-1202980836420001@192.168.1.254>, gurman@ari.net (Joseph B. Gurman) writes:
>     Someone's going to have to invent their way out of Poincar�'s (c.
> 1900) and Lorenz's pointing out of chaotic results because of dependence
> on initial conditions. Making the grid smaller will _not_ lead to better
> forecasting, or longer-term forecasts, only to a wider variety of results.

Seems we've drifted off topic a bit.  With respect to the original
comment that extensive computing power will continue to be needed,
I have little doubt of that.  Over the years there have been many
predictions as to what the most computing power we would ever need
would be, and most of them are looking rather silly these days.

But I also have some opinions on the weather forecasting issue.

Chaos theory is widely used to suggest that we are nearing ultimate
limits in our ability to predict and control nature, but I am not
sure that that is in fact what we need to conclude from it.

At any rate, improved computational accuracy and mesh sizes should
improve things assuming we also improve our measurements. Of course
it could just be the case that the instrumentation to make the
measurements needed to predict the weather one more week in the
future could be so large as to cause its own weather.

If the equations are in fact correct and the initial conditions are
in fact precisely measured, we would of course theoretically be
able to compute the weather given enough computational power no
matter how chaotic the solution is.  But in reality the equations
are wrong - they don't reach down to the quantum domain, for example;
they don't reflect the behavior of living beings; terms are omitted
in order to simplify calculations; and so on.

So it's not just chaos theory that's limiting us.

A counterbalance to chaos theory, though, is the implication that a
system which is sensitive to changes in initial conditions may just be
a system whose behavior is readily modified.  Work out the calculations
in fine enough detail and you may find that you can choose whether or
not any particular hurricane develops two weeks from now.

We certainly will not start out by controlling weather, but there
are systems which can be coaxed relatively easily into avoiding
chaotic operational modes, and chaos theory can help us understand
how to do this in increasingly complex environments.  Who knows but
what one day we'll have quasi-synthetic weather in the sense not of
attempting to create it but in the sense of making it more
predictable.

I can see it now: simulate the weather a month in advance; then
as the weather starts to deviate from the simulation, apply subtle
weather modification tactics to bring it back in line with what
was predicted.  In this way, you would not be accused of modifying
the weather to suit your own needs - you would just be letting
the computer simulation of the weather _be_ the weather.

--
George Cornelius              cornelius@eisner.decus.org
                              cornelius@mayo.edu

 [As if I know anything about weather simulation.  I detested every
 course in differential equations I ever took, so I suppose I should
 be welcoming claims of their imminent demise rather than predicting
 continued success.]



