In article <34e5ed9c.93978@news.demon.co.uk>, alan@mullen.demon.co.uk (Alan Campbell) writes:
:I have just started programming in C on our VAX running Open VMS 6.2.  I have
:written a couple of functions that I want to store in our object library, which
:currently contains functions written in Basic, but can not find any way of
:setting the version number of the function in the object library.  We use the
:%IDENT compiler directive in basic to specifiy the version number of our basic
:functions.   Is there an equivelent complier directive in C ?

  Under the DEC C compiler, the directive is:

    #pragma module ...

  Under the VAX C compiler, the directive is:

    #module ...

  Please consider looking in the C documentation, in the C help library,
  and in example directories such as SYS$EXAMPLES:*.C, before posting a
  question.  (The syntax of the C preprocessor directives is included in
  the DEC C help library, with the full details of the directives in the
  DEC C documentation.)  And if you do not have access to the C compiler
  documentation, you will want to acquire it in hardcopy or on CD-ROM.

	--

  If running VAX C, please upgrade to DEC C -- the most recent version of
  VAX C was released about a decade ago; it predates ANSI C standards.
  DEC C V5.6 is the most recently shipped release, but I'd expect to see
  a new release in the near future.  The most recent version of VAX C is
  V3.2, and there have been many releases of the C compiler between V3.2
  and V5.6.  And iif you have a "C" license PAK, you can have both VAX C
  and DEC C installed and in use on the same OpenVMS VAX system....


