Pete Dickerson wrote:
> 
<snip>
> >Now some of the Alpha cheerleaders seem to believe Alpha will have a vast
> >performance advantage over Merced when Merced ships. If so, that will be a
> >stunning lack of execution on Intel's part. Which could happen and it would
> >be a very interesting time in the market. But I'm certainly not betting any
> >money on Intel screwing up.
> >
> Let us not forget that there is a hard lower limit to Merced performance.
> IA-64 has to deliver better performance than an IA-32 processor at
> introduction (at least x2 if there is a price penalty). I would not be
> surprised to see 40 specint95 from non Intel products in 1999. Floating
> point may be another story though.
> Intel may be talking down IA-64 performance - they don't want other to rush
> to keep up.
> --

I should hope that Alpha has a damned substantial performance over the
initial release of IA64. If not, it will be a stunning lack of execution
on DEC's (or Samsung's) part. Only Intel knows for sure what the
performance on IA64 will be, but Conventional Wisdom has it in the 40-60
SPECint95 range. 

The soon-to-ship EV6 will deliver ~40SPECint95 at 600MHz. By the end of
this year, EV67 should be available at a higher clock rate, new process,
and correspondingly better performance.

By the end of 1999, DEC (or Samsung) will be shipping EV68 and EV7 parts
that should deliver ~70SPECint95 and ~100SPECint9r respectively.

I have little doubt that Alpha will maintain a substantial performance
advantage now and in the future. And with the world embracing IA64,
Alpha offers CPQ an attractive way to differentiate its enterprise
servers from everyone else's IA64-based offerings.

Alpha didn't catch on at the low end due to apps availability and
utterly squatulent marketing. Standardization rules at the low end:
