gurman@ari.net (Joseph B. Gurman) wrote:

>     Someone's going to have to invent their way out of Poincar�'s (c.
> 1900) and Lorenz's pointing out of chaotic results because of dependence
> on initial conditions. Making the grid smaller will _not_ lead to better
> forecasting, or longer-term forecasts, only to a wider variety of results.

Sensitivity to initial conditions (in layman's terms: chaotic behaviour) and 
accuracy due to finer grids are two different animals.  The first problem is 
not solvable in general - it will always be with us; however, with the 
current use of meteorological observation systems, there's still progress to 
be made.  I.c. for Western Europe, the best thing to do is to move 
observations from land to sea (the Atlantic), because that'll ensure you of 
better initial conditions upstream.

Finer grids enable you to follow fast moving systems better.  See Numerical 
Recipes Chapter 19.0 and 19.1 for an introduction to this issue.

IOW: Give me a (or better: several) 21264's any day !

