Actually, the Alpha apps picture looks quite a bit better than it did a
year ago. Apps are being added to DUNIX at the rate of 30 per week; an
aggregate of 85 new apps are ported to Alpha each week.

Granted, Alpha's total of ~11,500 or so apps across 3 OSes is less than
the total on Solaris alone, but the porting rate is quite substantial. I
understand that DEC has made 4K development systems available to ISVs,
which might account for the uptake in porting.

> 
> To me it looks like Compaq will just amortize (i.e let die off) all of
> Dec's technologies that don't fit into its desktop PC/NT server business
> model.

I still believe it's too early to say for certain. It is of course
incumbent on CPQ to state its direction, but they are muzzled until the
deal is finalized.

On the other hand, CPQ's ambition clearly transcends the existing
desktop PC/NT server space. CPQ wants ENTERPRISE to be its middle name.

> 
> I read something last week about Samsung possibly buying the Alpha.
> That would be interesting since Samsung has the fab plants to produce
> the chip and the brains, one assumes, to sell it OEM instead of trying
> to build high-margin proprietary workstations out of it.

Samsung now has an architectural license. The possibility of an outright
buy-out can't be written off. And like you said, Samsung has the
cerebral horsepower necessary to market the chip. Such was not the case
with the chip's current owner.

> 
> I was at a presentation given by Intel's VP in charge of the Merced and
> he said there will be life for the IA-32 after Merced.  The Merced, it
> seems, will be targeted at the lower-volume, higher-margin
> server/workstation market.  Meaning we will be able to buy 21264-like
> performance from Intel over a year after we can already get it from DEC,
> and at a premium too!

Architectural transitions **always** take time. Look at HP's really ugly
migration from CISC to PA-RISC. And DEC's VAX-Alpha migration. And even
Sun's SunOS to Solaris migration. (I would not be lulled by HP's claims
of a painless migration from PA-RISC to IA64.)

Accordingly, Intel would be foolish to discontinue IA32 when IA64 first
starts shipping.

The ancient Chinese curse "May you live in interesting times" says it
all.

