> But you can do this anyway, with something like the Alpha
> architecture, provided you have enough registers to store the
> intermediate results of both branches.  You just go ahead and
> compute both sides of the branch, and at the point where you
> know that your original condition will have been resolved, you
> ditch one half or the other.  That could be with a conditional
> move if only one value is involved, or a branch.  I'm afraid
> that I don't see how predication helps at all, really.

Part of the answer you stated yourself: "provided you have enough
registers...".  When software pipelining a loop, for example, 
register pressure can build up *quickly*, so full predication
helps by allowing reuse of temp registers in mutually exclusive
paths.  

The other possibly minor point is that a conditional move takes
additional time and code space.  (Note I said minor).  I don't know if 
this is an issue for a superscalar, OOO machine like Alpha.  On a
statically scheduled VLIW, extra moves can increase the recurrance
bound of a loop, leading to loop schedules at less than optimal II's. 

Finally, depending on how the predication works, it can eliminate
concerns about exceptions and pipeline stalls on not-taken paths.

-Reid




