The success of the x86 has (at least for the last decade or so) rested
not on it's technical merits or speed, but on it's application base- the
x86 has 18 gazillion applications, applications people are familiar with.
However, the critical applications, the ones everyone thinks of when
they say "but all the applications are on Windows/x86" have Microsoft on
them- and I think the actions of Microsoft will determine the futures of
both chips.

If Microsoft ports it's applications to the Alpha as native applications,
then come Merced, Alpha will have a (very good) shot at taking over the
top spot.  People are switching processors anyways, and Word is available
on both (I'm assuming here that of course MS will port to Merced), they'll
tend to go for the one with better performances.

If Microsoft does not port it's applications to the Alpha, but does port
them to Merced, the Alpha will at best continue it's nitch market as a
server CPU.  Although I could, in this instance, see Merced killing the
Alpha- as the performance advanatages of the Alpha would be outweighed
by that comforting warning sticker "Intel Inside" (think sheep mentality
here).  Alpha is doing as well as it is in the Sever market because it
can do things that the x86 can't- like have more than a few gig of memory.
Merced with also supply the larger address spaces, the better performance
than the x86, and all this with Intel Inside.

If Microsoft goes the lazy route and doesn't port it's applications to
either, both chips will be niche chips, and the x86 will continue along
(for a while).

Yes, it's annoying that the decision is being made by a software company,
and that it's being made probably on some combination of politics and
how difficult it'd be to port the code to 64 bit clean, instead of on
sound technical reasons.  That is, unfortunatly, the way the world works.


