In article <34E304A2.E18B39D@itap.physik.uni-stuttgart.de>,
Franz Gaehler  <gaehler@itap.physik.uni-stuttgart.de> wrote:
>    typedef unsigned long int * * Bag;
>    #define ADDR_OBJ        PTR_BAG
>    #define PTR_BAG(bag)    (*(Bag**)(bag))

>I suspect that the behaviour of cc should be called a bug. Or is this 
>the expected behaviour? Are there any fixes or work arounds known?

Yeah, I'd call it a bug as well. Doesn't happen with the old (OSF) C compiler,
does happen with the DEC C compiler (-migrate on older systems).

A work-around would be:

#define ADDR_OBJ(x)	PTR_BAG(x)

Another work-around would be to run your program through cpp directly, rather
than let the C compiler do it. You may want to pass the -no_cpp flag to the
C compiler to not bother trying to expand it again, but since the pre-
processing is built in it probably doesn't save you anything.

Another bug I found is that it doesn't swallow pre-processor directives if
the line before it has a continuation (actually, it was a bug in my code,
but the DEC C compiler didn't catch it; found it using gcc):

#if 0
#define WOOF woof \
#else
#define XYZ xyz
#endif

WOOF
XYZ

SHOULD produce:

WOOF
XYZ

DOES produce:

WOOF
xyz



