"Jeffrey J. Potoff" <jpotoff@rhea.umd.edu> writes:

>I have a Fortran 90 code where on occasion I need to take the
>exponential of some large numbers (500+).  As you can imagine,
>the result is a floating point overflow with real*8.

That's normal.

>I was able
>to avoid the floating overflow by going real*16, but now the 
>code is incredibly slow.

That's normal.  The real*16 gives you the extra dynamic range of a
longer exponent, but also the extra accuracy of a longer mantissa.
Your code works that much harder to compute exponentials to that
accuracy.

Go back to the math behind the code, factor out the exponent offset
analytically, and re-code.

