In article <6btivi$hna@news.or.intel.com>,
Jonathan C Hall <jchall@ichips.intel.com> wrote:
>Andrew Reilly (reilly@zeta.org.au) wrote:
>: But you can do this anyway, with something like the Alpha
>: architecture, provided you have enough registers to store the
>: intermediate results of both branches.  You just go ahead and
>: compute both sides of the branch, and at the point where you
>: know that your original condition will have been resolved, you
>: ditch one half or the other.  That could be with a conditional
>: move if only one value is involved, or a branch.  I'm afraid
>: that I don't see how predication helps at all, really.
>:
>
>I am by no means an expert on predication, but I think that I can
>answer this.
>
>The method you propose will not work if there are instructions on
>the speculative path that could cause traps or exceptions.  I don't
>know about Alpha, but on x86, there are many instructions that can
>produce exceptions.  This makes it very difficult for the compiler
>to insert optimizations involving a conditional move.  

on a typical risc chip running floating point code with exceptions
enabled, this could be a problem.  for integer code, most alu-type
operations don't cause exceptions.  for integer instructions which
can cause exceptions, you'd need an extra cmov to move in a safe
operand when the instruction is on the "wrong path" (e.g., cmov a 1
as the divisor when a divide isn't supposed to be done).  this, of
course, makes the code even bigger.  another solution is to add
non-faulting versions of instructions and demand software check the
validity of the data (e.g., the non-faulting loads in sparc-v9).

from what little i know of x86, it does sound like these sorts
of optimizations would be really, really hard.  but with only
8 registers, it's probably pointless to begin with.

andrew essen
essen@computer.org




