Paul DeMone wrote:
>     The 21264 contains 15.2 million transistors of which about 6 million
>     are in CPU logic (i.e. non-cache, ).  Compare this against the Intel
>     Pentium-II P6 CPU core which has less than 4.5 million transistors even
>     with the baggage of MMX and the x86 front end uOP decoders.

But since the 21264 has four integer and two FP units, this seems to be
replicated parts - the development costs don't scale with transistors,
they scale with complexity. If you have twice as many reservation
stations, you certainly have twice as many transistors, but it's a
simple cut&paste act to get there. And although dynamic OOO scheduling
takes many chip resources, the algorithm behind is simple*; especially
if the instruction set fits in well, and the Alpha ISA does.

However, it's not so difficult to make a rough estimation of the
development costs. I assume they have several hundret men, working for
some years (3 or so) on that project. That gives about 1000 man-years,
and thus is in the range of $100-$200 million.

*) Should I sketch it out? Well, each instruction is inserted into a
buffer, and may be executed, when both sources become available. Let's
assume that you have as many shadow registers as reservation stations.
Each instruction's destination register is allocated as new register,
and marked clean. So basically, if you insert a new instruction into the
reservation station, you translate the two source registers, and
allocate a destination register, marking it as clean. Source register
translation goes through RAM access for the older registers, and must be
overridden by those registers that have been newly allocated in the same
cycle due to "read after write"-"conflicts" in the decode stage
(resolving the conflicts is simple - the result, the new destination
register, already is available - you can allocate them before renaming
the source registers).

Even the actual scheduling can be done before the results are available
- if the latency is known (which it is most of the time), or if the
scheduling can be aborted if it is unknown, e.g. a potential cache miss.
Then, one instruction slot is lost per cache miss.

-- 
