Summary: 18000 billion elliptic curve operations
Organization: I.N.R.I.A Rocquencourt
Keywords: elliptic curve discrete logarithm record
Mime-Version: 1.0
Newsgroups: comp.sys.dec


Hello everybody,

We have successfully completed the calculation of over 18000 billion
elliptic curve operations to solve the ECC2-89 problem from Certicom's
challenge list.

Here is a summary of the four records we have set so far for the most
difficult elliptic curve discrete logarithm computations:

        Problem         Iterations      # of people     Time
        solved          (billions)                      (days)
    --------------------------------------------------------------
        ECCp-79          1400            2              30
        ECC2-79          1700 (*)       25               8
        ECCp-89         30000           57              24
        ECC2-89         18000 (*)       70              26

(*): note that each iteration for ECC2-79 and ECC2-89 was roughly
     twice as difficult as for ECCp-79 and ECCp-89.


We are a team of volunteers around the 'net, and anyone can help to
set the fifth record (fingers crossed):

        ECCp-97         ???             ???             ???


If you would to join us, you can get the source code for Alpha
workstations at:

  http://pauillac.inria.fr/~harley/ecdl4/


and we will put a 32-bit version there in the coming days.
There are also some statistics on our progress at:

  http://www.labs.bt.com/projects/security/crackers/p97/


The code was written by:

  Robert Harley (that's me) from the Cristal project at INRIA,
  John Sager and Adrian Escott from British Telecom Labs.

and the statistics are being done by Dimitris Tsapakidis from B.T.Labs.


Appended below is the submission we made to Certicom concerning ECC2-89.
They have confirmed that our answer is correct and have some
information about the challenge and our solutions at:

  http://www.certicom.ca/chal/contents.htm
  http://www.certicom.ca/first.htm
  http://www.certicom.ca/second.htm
  http://www.certicom.ca/third.htm
  http://www.certicom.ca/fourth.htm


We hope you'll join us soon!
Bye,
  Rob.

------------------------------------------------------------------------------
This message is copyright (c) Robert J. Harley, 1998.
If you wish to quote more than one sentence, please quote the whole thing.

To: certicom-ecc-challenge@certicom.com

                                        Robert J. Harley,
                                        S&egrave;vres, France,
                                        7th of February, 1998.

Dear Mr. Gallant,

The solution to Certicom's ECC2-89 problem is the residue class of
41871609686648820507900581 modulo 309485009821357445894232317.  The
calculation was carried out in 26 days by a group of 70 people in 17
countries.  95% of the work was done on Alpha workstations running
Linux and Digital Unix and the remaining 5% was done on various 32-bit
machines.

The fastest, naturally, were 600 MHz Alpha systems doing 241 K
elliptic curve operations per second each.  The fastest 32-bit systems
were 233 MHz StrongARM NCs running NetBSD at 55 K each.  Several other
systems contributed too including a bunch of Pentium and Pentium Pro
machines with Linux, a few Sparcs with SunOS, a 150 MHz SGI MIPS with
Irix, an old 80 Mhz HP PA with NextStep and a Cyrix 486 DX2.  Last and
definitely least were my trusty old 8 MHz ARM 2's running RISC OS
(hey, they seemed fast ten years ago :).

The people involved were:

          Wayne Baisley                    Greg Lindahl               
         Miguel Barreiro Paz              Brian Lund                  
            Uri Blumenthal                Preda Mihailescu            
         Spider Boardman               Francois Morain                
          Alvin Brattli                    Pete Murray                
           Bill Broadley                    Jon Nathan                
        Andries Brouwer                Burkhard Neidecker-Lutz        
           Zach Brown                    Wieger Opmeer                
          Bruce Dawson                    Vance Petree                
       Dr. Sven Dietrich              Guillaume Pierre                
          Einar Doerum                   Martin Radford               
        Dragisa Duric                       Jon Reeves                
         Martin Edu                       Brian Romansky              
           Gwyn Evans                    Geordy Rostad                
        Douglas Frank                       Tim Rowley                
          Megan Gentry                   Andrew Sapozhnikov           
           Rick Gorton                    Aaron Sawyer                
         Thomas Gschwind                   Mike Schloss               
           Oleg Gusev                        Al Simons                
        Mikolaj Habryn                    Mikko Siren                 
         Robert Harley                    Chris Smith                 
          David Hauan                      Mark Smith                 
           Mike Iglesias                 Murray Stokely               
       Chatchai Jantaraprim       Adrian En-Wei Sun                   
         Travis Johnson                   Peter Sward                 
         Martin Kahlert                   Marko Vendelin              
           Asim Kepkep                     Paul Verwer                
          Rohit Khare                      Bill Viggers               
           Mika Kortelainen            Bart-Jan Vrielink              
        Andreas Krall                       Dan Weeks                 
         Edward Lee                     Michael Wins                  
   Dr. Hiankiat Lee                         Tom Woodburn              
           Leon Lessing                 Gregory Woodbury              

and the British Telecom team, some students of the Ecole Centrale de Lille
and a person who prefers to remain anonymous.

The method we used was a "birthday paradox" algorithm iterating from a
random initial point (one per machine) with a pseudo-random function
(the same on all machines) until a collision was detected at 16:21
today.

A total of 18161819582507 iterations i.e., over 18000 billion, were
performed finding 17543 "distinguished" points.  Two of the points,
found by Guillaume Pierre of INRIA and Bill Broadley of U.C.Davis,
were in fact equal allowing us to compute the final answer.  Since an
ECC2-89 iteration took close to twice as long as an ECCp-89 iteration,
this was the most difficult calculation we have done so far.

Participants at INRIA found 3653 points using machines belonging to
the following projects: Air, Algo, Codes, Coq, Cristal, M&eacute;val,
Para, Sor and Sosso.  Those at Digital found 4591 points, and others
found 9299.

Our source code can be downloaded from:

  http://pauillac.inria.fr/~harley/ecdl3/


We invite anyone interested in working on the next calculation to
point their Web browsers at:

  http://pauillac.inria.fr/~harley/ecdl4/


Bye,
  Rob.
