> So many of the things that UNIX server shops take for granted are
> missing from NTS.
>
> Lack of standard sensible backup software
> Nothing like Jumpstart
> Nothing like CacheFS or AutoFS
> No proper hierachical name service
> Poor remote management capabilites
> Lamentable scalability
> Shutdown and restart for all but the most minor configuration changes
> A DNS implementation that is so good that Microsoft use a UNIX DNS
> server themselves.

Wow!  Back in the 1980s, UNIX was this upstart operating system that ran
on minicomputers, and the mainframe people told us that UNIX would never
take off because it lacked features like the above - management, Big Iron, etc., etc.

Now the same arguments are being applied to NT.

The real question is not whether these features can be applied to NT like they were
applied to UNIX, nor whether NT will catch up with UNIX the way UNIX has almost
caught up with mainframes.

The real question is whether the single company, Microsoft, can be as innovative as
the multiple companies (basically SUN, HP, and AT&T/UNIXware, and, to a lesser extent,
Tandem, Amdahl, etc.) were in the UNIX OS market.






