    Even before Alpha came out I have been continuously amazed how very little a
    significant performance advantage *can* count for in determining commercial
    success even in the most notoriously "hot-box" oriented markets like technical
    workstations.  Look at how Sun was able to expand its market share in the "dark
    years" of the SuperSPARC when it was in a laughable performance position versus
    most other RISC vendors.

    The other thing about performance advantages is how temporary they are given
    the continuous progress in semiconductor technology.  When the 0.5 um, 300 MHz
    21164 came out in mid 1995 it was a technical marvel, nearly doubling performance
    on the next fastest device (the 21064A incidently).  Now less than three years
    later I can go to a department store and buy a relatively cheap 233 MHz PII
    system that can easily outperform that 21164 on SPECint95. 

    Intel's main business problem now isn't that Pentium II is slower in the
    workstations and servers than some RISC based machines.  Intel is primarily
    worried that there isn't any compelling reason for most people with 200 MHz
    P55 boxes to upgrade to PIIs, and that under $1000 PCs are now over a third
    of the market.
    
    It is quite sad and ironic that Alpha is possibly posed for its greatest
    technical successes at the same time it seems to be in its most precarious
    position as far as an ongoing business   :-(

