Bill Broadley <bill@math.ucdavis.edu> writes:

> 
> In comp.arch Paul DeMone <PaulDeMone@EasyInternet.net> wrote:
> : to 16 KB and 32 KB respectively.  This shows in the increased transistor count, 5.7
> 
> I'm curious, what workloads/applications is a 32k I cache and 16k D cache
> designed for?  Seems like traditionally if it's not split usually it's the
> D cache that gets bigger.  I.e. the HP 730.

If I remember correctly, the answer lies in the minium page size for
the alpha architecture.  The original ev5 had 8k pages because they
were the largest page size that could be done using only the pa bits
of the va to index the cache.  For pca56 they were able to double the
icache easily.  When it became time for pca57 they obviously need
larger caches since they were operating at higher frequencies.  Since
the Dcache needs to be dual ported it is bigger per bit than the
icache.  With that in mind I would guess that they alocated the same
die area to both the i and d caches and the icache simply could fit
twice as must real data in the area.

aaron spink
