In article <34E11E9E.5994@easyinternet.net>,
	Paul DeMone <PaulDeMone@EasyInternet.net> writes:
> Andrew Essen wrote:
> [snip] 
>> assuming adding full predication was free, it's theoretically superior
>> since the instruction counts are lower.  but what additional optimizations
>> does it enable (that are precluded by using only conditional moves)?
>> it's certainly a neat concept, but not a panacea.
> 
>    The main benefit of predication is that it allows many conditional branches
>    to be eliminated.  This allows basic blocks (a code segment that is entered
>    from the beginning and exits from the end; this is maximum portion of code
>    that many compiler optimizations can be applied to) to be coalesced into
>    larger basic blocks.  This opens up the code to further optimizations and
>    more efficient scheduling, especially on wide issue machines.

But you can do this anyway, with something like the Alpha
architecture, provided you have enough registers to store the
intermediate results of both branches.  You just go ahead and
compute both sides of the branch, and at the point where you
know that your original condition will have been resolved, you
ditch one half or the other.  That could be with a conditional
move if only one value is involved, or a branch.  I'm afraid
that I don't see how predication helps at all, really.

>    the present discussion since IA-64 evaluates predicate conditions into
>    one (or two?) of 64 one bit predicate flags rather than a general purpose
>    register.

I don't think that the predicate register was the issue, rather
than the storage required for multiple instances of the
variables undergoing "Schrodinger's cat" computations.

