This is my personal experience of runnig Adobe Photoshop 4.0 on Alpha 533Mhz
(PC164LX) workstation:
The software ran very very slow for the first time especially for huge 122MB
images, the speed was comparable to/ or slower than an old Dell Pentium 233Mhz
computer that my counterpart was using.  The bottlenecks were most obvious  in
three areas: blowing up the images to 122MB, filtering the huge 122MB images,
and saving the 122Mb image.  The performance for these features is spectacular
on a Dual Pentium II 300Mhz workstation. But I never gave up on Alpha, I ran
the Phtoshop commands for a few times and optimised the code manually.  And
these are the results, beating the performance of the Dual Pentium II 300Mhz
workstations:

Dual Pentium II 300Mhz (with 32MB graphics, 256MB RAM, UW SCSI HDD)
--------------------------
Blowing up image: 41 secs
Filtering the image (Gaussian Blur, 122MB): about 3-4 minutes
Saving the image: more than 12 minutes

Alpha 533Mhz (with 16MB graphics, 384MB RAM, UW SCSI 5400rpm HDD)
----------------------------
Before optimisation:
Blowing up image: more than 5 minutes
Filtering the image (Gaussina Blur, 122MB): About 30 minutes, seems forever
though.
Saving the Image: About 8 minutes.

After Optimisation:
Blowing up the image: 35 secs.
Filtering the image: sligthly more than 2 minutes
Saving the image: less than a minute!

It might have to do with the higher RAM I used on the Alpha workstation, but I
think the performance is more than acceptable for Alpha running Intel based
application and considering the fact that Photoshop is optimised for Dual P
computers.


Best Regards.




