Andrew Reilly (reilly@zeta.org.au) wrote:
: I'm afraid that I don't see how predication helps at all, really.

The way I see it, special support for predication buys one support for
fined grained predication (small numbers of instructions) without costing
any extra issue slots. The cost for this is more bits dedicated to each
issue slot. Obviously this only makes sense if one plans to use a lot of
predication.

I tend to agree with the position that predication can be used with
existing architectures (perhaps more effectively with implementation level
support). As such its a lot like all the compiler techniques that were
commonplace in RISC compilers which got used to improve x86 (68k, VAX,
etc.) performance as well. (Which fed into the decisions on what to
optimize for the next generation of those CISCs.) The same fundamental
phenomena that helped the x86 can also be used by existing RISCs if they so
choose.

I disagree that there is no difference between how Alpha does predication
and how Merced will do it. (Based on publically available info about each.)
As always, the details matter.

-Z-



