Well, the dust has settled, everyone's had their $0.20 worth. Here's another
two bob's worth.

I still urge OpenVMS engineers (past & present) to formulate a backup plan
to buy out VMS business should COMPAQ prove unable & unwilling. Under such a
scenario:

- run a global business with web-servers to disseminate software, patches,
etc
- UPS, FedExpress, DHL to deliver parts globally (maybe from strategic hubs)
- foster cottage industry of garage developers - "freebie-cheap" desktop
platform with compilers, APIs, etc; disseminated off internet servers
- allow any developer to flourish (& suitably rewarded)- encourage them to
develop on cheap desktop that will run on the enterprise, galaxy systems
(original IBM-PC business model)
- yes! Virginia, new world business model; give away front end "freebies"
that can be turned into value-added products for your back-end system -
those who cannot see it are called Digital Management, I believe.
- frontend "freebie" users will have to rely on Decus, Internet newsgroups
for support unless they want to pay serious money like MCI or they've been
elevated by virtue of producing the next killer app.
- your sole focus is to ensure that OpenVMS+Alpha is the top platform bar
none; a bit like building the biggest stadiums, public malls and encourage
everyone to come together to interact, cooperate, build & thrive under the
most secure & stable environment, period!
- contract assembly of systems in manufacturing(like Sun in their early days
with immigrant labour gangs)
- Alpha chips can be sourced from Samsung, Mitsubishi and ASIC foundries -
since COMPAQ is too stupid to see the value of Alpha, they'll probably pay
you to take the chip off their hands - remember: hypothetical scenario :)
- I believe some new Alpha systems ship with X25/telephone access to
console; so you can resolve client problems anywhere in the world; unless of
course you sold one to the penquins - that's ok, we can cover the antarctic
for you in Australia.

there! all you need is to cross the t's, dot the i's and take it to your
bankers.

*Note: The PDP-11 still thrives today despite DEC's effort to kill it -
there will always be a company with no ambitions to be #1, but to run a
profitable business catering to knowledgeable customers like PDP-11 users.

Now, a bit on the threat of NT, Unix. "They will always be found wanting!"
why?

The disappearing horizon effect.
As NT & unix strive for scalability & reliability to handle today's
"supposedly" large workload, the target keeps shifting beyond the horizon -
so what is considered mid-range workload today; will be desktop workload
beyond 2000- hence NT & unix will always be minnow, desktop platforms -
always found wanting!

There will always be bigger, newer problems to tackle; eg. weather
forecasting - four day forecasts are common now; but 7-day forecasts and
smaller grid (ie rain in one suburb, cloudy in another, sunny yet another)
is being proposed - means even more computational grunt. The same applies
for CAD/CAM, FEA, real-time global trading with instantaneous risk analysis,
margin-clearance, authorisation; realistic movie animation, and so on -
never ever enough CPU power to solve ever more bigger problems. Those who
cannot see it have "Club of Rome" mentality. Those so called "thinkers"
assembled in Rome back in 19th Century(?) & predicted that by 1960, there
will be so many horse-drawn carriages, the streets of London will be covered
in 2ft of dung; some spoil-sport had to invent the motor car.

So what's the point, you ask? Well, the Alpha allows mankind to push the
boundary of discovery & advancement much faster - more importantly, cheaply
enough for more(garage developers) to have a go instead of just white-coated
scientists looking for quarks, positrons or whatever.

It would be a tragedy for humanity if COMPAQ drops the Alpha & the world
allows it to slide by; we'll never be able to discover warp speed & get off
this planet, away from troglodyte IBMers, Sun's shit... There's also this
natural biological law that requires diversity in gene pool for species to
adapt, thrive and advance.

ok, dokee, I'll vacate the soap-box now, ;)
