> :The TLZ09 is on our Alphaserver 1000A.   It is a replacement
> :for a series of TLZ07 drives which were failing at an alarming
> :rate.   It has now been a month since it was installed, with
> :zero errors or problems other than this compatibility issue.
> :
> :The tapes are 3M (Imation) DDS-90.   These particular tapes
> :were first used in August 1997, and have been used about seven
> :times (BACKUP/VERIFY).
> :
> :I have no problems writing tapes on the Archive drives and
> :reading them on the TLZ09.
> :
> :Has anyone else experienced this problem?   Any ideas?
> 
>   I would first look for SCSI problems, which can include cabling that is
>   too long, too many SCSI connections, improper termination, and poor or
>   loose cabling.  I would start with the assumption that the SCSI bus is
>   too long, and work from there -- any SCSI bus, almost by definition, is
>   too long for correct operation.  I would then look for CPU, bus, memory,
>   controller, and tape errors (using DECevent).
> 

I'll vouch for the SCSI bus problems ... Alan knows of my many trips to
Phoenix to fix a data corruption problem that turned out to be the
result of cheap SCSI cables ... there were 3 common ground wires for the
25 signal wires. :-(  And then tehre were too many connectors.

One of the best things to do for SCSI cables is to stay with Ribbon
cables, or approx 200 ohm twisted pair cables, as few connectors as
possible, and preferably not the huge Centronics style  printer type
connectors ... These add wicked reflections on even short cables.

Stuart



