Burkhard Neidecker-Lutz wrote:
> 
> In article <34DA9AF0.1508@EasyInternet.net> Paul DeMone <PaulDeMone@EasyInternet.net> writes:
> >   On the other hand IA-64 can eliminate somewhat more than half of mispredicted
> >   branches from predication conversion of branches.  The 21264 has a quite
> >   powerful hybrid/adaptive branch prediction scheme but despite this, mis-
> >   predicted branches costs about 0.1 CPI, or about 20% of the 0.5 CPI that
> >   the '264 gets running SPEC95 (according to MDR).  So in theory just the
> >   predication feature of the IA-64 ISA could provide a >10% boost in perf-
> >   ormance for a CPU implementation similar in resources and issue width as
> >   the 21264 at a given clock frequency.
> 
> Wait a second. You are blindly quoting the 50% figure from the predication
> paper. The 50% figure is
> 
> a) relative to a simple 2 bit counter predictor (i.e. the
>    adaptive pattern predictor in the 21264 gets more right)

    Who says that Merced cannot use a similarly advanced branch prediction algorithm?
    The benefit of predication is the elimination of roughly half of conditional
    branches, not necessarily a change in the predictability of branches.
> 
> b) you are assuming that the Digital compilers don't perform IF-conversion
>    for certain code patterns using the predication features of the Alpha-ISA.
>    (which they do with the -speculate flags).

    Look at the code generated by aggressive predicate based compilation in the papers
    that describe these techniques.  The code quality depends a lot on predicating
    general arithmetic and logic instructions and predicate setting instructions
    themselves.  The presence of just a conditional move instruction is a quite pale
    imitation of a fully predicated ISA.

> 
> A lot of the good features of the IA64 are so good that they
> are already in Alpha...

    I hope so.  Unfortunately if good features are present in both IA-64 and Alpha
    in which form will the majority of the great unwashed masses will buy them?

    Alpha has had poor results competiting in a battle of architectural wits against
    an unarmed opponent, x86;  Is this situation going to get better against a new
    design that benefits from a) examining Alpha's strength's and weaknesses, and b)
    exploiting advances in architecture/compiler design since Alpha was laid down?

