Organization: UUNET Canada News Transport
Mime-Version: 1.0
Newsgroups: comp.sys.dec,comp.arch


Perhaps lost in the silliness surrounding IBM's toy 1 GHz processor, an apparently
improved version of the Alpha 21164PC was described at ISSCC in the paper "A 667MHz
RISC Microprocessor Containing a 6.0ns 64b Integer Multiplier" by D. Carlson et al.

The new 21164PC like device is made in a 0.28 um four level metal CMOS process as
opposed to the 0.35 um orginal.  This gives a clock rate of 667 MHz (or higher)
compared to 533 MHz for the original along with lower power consumption, 22 W vs
39 W.  The branch prediction hardware seems to be improved with the BHT doubled in
size and the addition of global history bits (gshare ?).  The icing on the cake is
the fact that the on chip data and instruction caches have each been doubled in size
to 16 KB and 32 KB respectively.  This shows in the increased transistor count, 5.7
million vs 3.5 million.  Finally, the new 21164PC should be even cheaper since the
die area is reduced from 138 mm2 to 100 mm2.

This is good news for the competitiveness of low cost Alpha systems.  The new Alpha
CPU will likely use the 166 MHz burst SRAMs being ramped up for the 333 MHz slot 1
Deschutes.  The new device should see a small but noteworthy increase in performance
from the increased L1 cache size in addition to the delta from the higher clock rate
(as opposed to the Klamath to Deschutes PII shrink which kept the L1 cache sizes the
same).  Maybe DEC can even run this part at half speed and reduced core voltage (like
the mobile Deschutes) to have a CPU suitable for laptops  ;-)


Paul

All opinions strictly my own.

