Andrew Essen wrote:
[snip] 
> assuming adding full predication was free, it's theoretically superior
> since the instruction counts are lower.  but what additional optimizations
> does it enable (that are precluded by using only conditional moves)?
> it's certainly a neat concept, but not a panacea.

   The main benefit of predication is that it allows many conditional branches
   to be eliminated.  This allows basic blocks (a code segment that is entered
   from the beginning and exits from the end; this is maximum portion of code
   that many compiler optimizations can be applied to) to be coalesced into
   larger basic blocks.  This opens up the code to further optimizations and
   more efficient scheduling, especially on wide issue machines.

   Predication can also be split into unconditional and OR-types.  Uncond-
   itional predicates basically evaluate a single condition.  OR-type pred-
   icates allows multiple predicate setting instructions to be evaluated
   in the same cycle, again, particularly useful on a very wide machine.

> 
> i'm somewhat familiar with generic risc isa's and, for better or worse,
> pretty unfamiliar with ia64 except for the microprocessor forum slides.
> what i don't see here is what full predication of all instructions buys
> over partial predication other than reduced instruction counts.
> conditional loads, conditional alu-ops, and conditional stores can be
> done with small macros of 2-3 instructions using conditional moves.
> 
> personally, the more important ia64 feature for predication support appears
> to be the larger register files.

   The register file size is not particularly relevent for predication in
   the present discussion since IA-64 evaluates predicate conditions into
   one (or two?) of 64 one bit predicate flags rather than a general purpose
   register.

> 
> andrew essen
> essen@computer.org

