Good Posting!!!!

I went to work @ E&M San Diego in the early 80's. Mid-80's the
useless HR apparatus announced a new recruiting initiative "64K".
At the time we thought it was "cute" - same time as the 64K memory
chips. But looking back - it was a scheme to de-populate the NCR
work force. Trim it to sixty-four thousand employees or less.

Dayton was primarily retail or financial. I was with their unprofitable
San Diego VRX computer division (unprofitable on the equipment sales;
money bags when considering service and support). But they
never looked at the "total" picture.

NCR had some good corporate management. But San Diego's
overhead  seemed to drift from one goof to another. We're
"gonna invest big"; "we're gonna define the computer
industry"....."we're gonna fund our new product strategy off
our dying mainframe revenue stream". First "red" quarter,
and another layoff. Layoff.....after layoff.....after layoff.
Twenty percent decrease in engineering talent (!layoff!), and a twenty
percent increase in UNPAID work hours (mandatory weekends).
Want Saturday off - take a vacation day!

So from a Dayton perspective - NCR was family. From a San Diego
view - layoff survival.

My only regret was not being laid off. I believed their bullshit; put in
the extra hours; traveled extensively; and now have only regrets for
wasting my time and efforts upon their failed visions.

Yes - B.T., J.C., S.S., M.R. - YOU SHOULD HAVE LAID ME OFF!
I couldn't help myself until I'd achieved bullshit overload! :-)

I escaped San Diego just prior to the corporate split (Sept '96), and the last
eighteen months have been fantastic.  Too bad Compaq didn't buy NCR. I known
one NCR location that the overhead needs to see the parking lot like many of
our project members did for the final time!

Mark C. - why did you abandon us in our darkest hour? You left before cleaning
house!

pgladen@mho.net



