Hoff Hoffman <hoffman@xdelta.enet.dec.nospam> wrote in article
<6bg6g8$6lp@usenet.pa.dec.com>...
> 
>   Rather than discussing the "on-LAN" capabilities of DECnet, what
>   are the general goal(s) involved here?

I'm thinking about using hierarchical Virtual LANs (OSI RM layer 2) to
segment the broadcast domain, yet maintaining DECnet routing between areas.

>   The "on-LAN" flag is a deliberate DECnet performance optimization.
> 
>   The "on-LAN" flag avoids transmitting the packet over the network
>   two (or more) times, and it also reduces the traffic and loading
>   on the intermediate router(s).

But in the case where inter-area communication is minimal but using a "flat
network" creates a very large broadcast domain can create issues (we are
talking about thousands of DECnet end-nodes)
 
>   I know of no way to disable this behaviour, short of the obvious
>   case involving the partitioning of the Ethernet LAN network -- so
>   that the end-nodes cannot see each other and must route all traffic
>   through an L1 or L2 router.
> 
>   As there are no security advantages to using multiple areas on a
>   single Ethernet or FDDI LAN, I am not certain what you are trying
>   to achieve here.  The only reason I can imagine for having multiple
>   areas on a LAN is around the additional available DECnet addresses.
>   And I would expect that a LAN with that many addresses present would
>   likely also need to be segmented anyway, due to the likely volume of
>   Ethernet traffic that would result from having roughly 1K (or more)
>   nodes present...

DECnet routers have only one MAC address, even with multiple interfaces.
This is fine with a pure DECnet network. But with a multi-protocol brouted
network, this can create forwarding problems (especially with virtual
LANs).

It's unfortunate to know that there is no way to do this. If this is indeed
the case, I'll have to find new ways.

rgds,
_Michael.
 




