In article <Pine.SOL.3.96.980206092319.15995C-100000@sol>, Hiker <x71ed@unb.ca> writes:
:Please help me: I'm a student programmer and need help on how to edit a
:binary data file on a vax work station... I have  a binary editor for dos
:but the conversion completely screws the file (Vax -> DOS)....anyway any
:tips would be greatly appreciated...

  You might want to explain what you are doing, and what sort of binary
  file you are looking to edit -- there can be good and bad reasons to
  do this, and there can be good and bad approaches to doing this, and
  there can be alternatives -- such as CONVERTing to a sequential file
  format, editing, and then reloading.

  PATCH is the most flexible file editor shipped with OpenVMS VAX -- it
  can modify any sort of disk file, though it is particularly designed
  to patch VAX executable image files.

  On the Freeware CD-ROM, one can aquire other tools for various roughly
  similar purposes, such as DISKBLOCK, RMS_TOOLS, etc.  I've also seen
  references to other patch-like tools -- tools that perform functions
  similar to PATCH -- posted here, see the http://www.dejanews.com/ site
  archives, or the Info-VAX archives.

	--

  File transfers between OpenVMS VAX -- VAX systems can run various
  operating systems, I'll assume OpenVMS is in use -- and Microsoft
  Windows 95 are generally performed via FTP, and corruptions can creep
  in when an incorrect FTP file transfer mode is used.  Image and binary
  files usually (surprise! :-) require the FTP image/binary transfer mode.


