> I take Andy's comments to mean "one can make many complex
> microarchitectural features run at high clock rates with suitable up front
> design." Which I'd hardly deny but it sure as hell doesn't look easy to do
> so. I am a little confused by the quote he attributed to someone at
> DEC. Are there specific types of circuits only used in OOO designs? Which
> are they?  Content associative memories?

Ummm....

The key to doing high speed OOO microarchitecture is to minimize the use
of Priority CAMs to read out data (at least data that needs to be read out quickly).

(Non-priority CAMs are reasonable for writes, including possibly multicast
writes, except for loading dependent on the number of receivers.)

For example, most of the original "Register Renaming" papers talked about using
complex CAMs to do the renaming, performing operations such as
"activate every entry in the history buffer that contains this logical register,
and then select the oldest version of it."

E.g. Patt, Hwu, et al's HPSm had a versioned register file that had to do such a prioritization
on register version tags.

Whereas the P6, and the 21264, so far as I can tell, basically make register renaming into a straightforward RAM register lookup, by
devoting a pipestage.

(Maybe this seems obvious now, but I had some pretty well-known people at AMD and Digital
tell me it was a stupid idea.)

---

So, I take the Digital guy's comment as pertaining to particular implementations,
of register renaming (and also scheduling), that implement high level microarchitecture
concepts in low level easy to make run fast logic design.

*Not* saying "throw circuit designers at it".





