Dan Pop wrote:
> 
> In <34DA9016.7673@world.std.com> Terry Shannon <shannon@world.std.com> writes:
> 
> >DEC has made more than three big mistakes, but here's the SKD take on
> >the Three Big Ones:
> ...
> >1992-8: Failing to market Alpha aggressively.
> 
> Make that:
> 
> 1985-90: Failing to become a *significant* presence on the Unix
>          workstation market.
> 
> With VMS slowly fading away and with Alpha/NT the poor sister of x86/NT,
> the success of Alpha on the market depended heavily on the Alpha/Unix
> workstation sales.  But it came at a time when the Unix workstation
> market belonged already to Sun and HP and few people/businesses were
> inclined to switch to a new hardware and software platform for a certain
> gain in speed.  The fact that Alpha/Unix was little endian when ALL its
> competitors on the Unix market (Sun, HP, IBM, SGI) were big endian was
> not a big incentive for changing platforms, either.
> 
> Alpha was a commercial "failure" because the boxes built around it had
> no significant market.  People don't buy processors.  The only realistic
> chance of success would have been an aggressively priced Alpha/Solaris
> platform (although I like Digital's Unix a lot more than Solaris).

Can't argue with any of the above. Note that when the entire family of
Alpha systems was announced--SANS SOFTWARE--on November 10, 1992; all
DEC managed to do was freeze the sales of VAXen.

And for what it's worth, the original Alpha game plan called for DEC to
restrict OS support on Alpha to OpenVMS for the first year or two. The
rationale: give VMS a big performance boost. Of course, said asinine
plan flew right in the face of the open systems marketing thrust, so it
was dumped.

