Sure, a modem can be used with a uVAX3100.  However, because the
uVAX3100 uses the 5 wire MMJ interface, it does not correctly support
the modem handshake lines.  Because of this, setting the port /MODEM may
not work.

You can set the port /NOMODEM, but this presents some security
problems.  If you will be dialing out and have disabled autoanswer on
the modem, this is not much of a problem.  But if you will have users
dialing in, I wouldn't recommend it.

At one time, I looked at patching the serial port driver to make modem
handshake work by misusing some of the MMJ lines.  I think this is
possible, but I ended up getting a DECserver 200/MC.  This is a much
more robust way to handle modems.

Good luck!


