References: <01bd32e4$8ee449e0$20a03195@athena> <34db3c5c.252584753@news.deco.com>
Organization: Hong Kong University of Science and Technology
Newsgroups: comp.sys.dec


In general, DECnet nodes are broadcasting a lot of hello messages. To keep
the broadcasting domain small, you need to have level 2 routers. But on
Ethernet, the routers in general, belongs to only one DECnet area. So
putting routers of different areas on the same "bridged" network, does not
limit the size of the broadcast domain. 

The second choice, as you have mentioned, is to put the systems on
different networks. But then, you need many dual-interface routers, or a
large multi-interface router. Either way is costly and clumsy (in the sense
that MAC addresses are tied to DECnet addresses, and can cause problems in
a multi-protocol brouted networks).

The rational behind me is that if the intra-Ethernet can be turned off,
there is a possibility to use hierarchical Virtual LANs so that Level 2
routers can route messages between themselves while end node broadcasts can
be contained only within the same area.

So let me repeat my question - Does any one know how to suppress
intra-Ethernet from Ethernet DECnet nodes ?

x-jsue@cs.indiana.edu wrote in article
<34db3c5c.252584753@news.deco.com>...
