Anita Hegerlend <foo@bar.com> wrote:
}I'm writing a program that executes other programs on a number of
}machines in the net. For that purpose I use the rexec() call that
}reports a socket descriptor from that I can read the outputs of the
}remotely started program and close the descriptor afterwards. In case
}that a machine cannot be reached, e.g. is switched off, the respective
}socket descriptor "gets lost" because a -1 (and no descriptor) is
}reported in order to report a problem. The outputs of the function
}(which is called within a pthread) show, that a descriptor that could
}have been used for testing the existence of an unreacheable host is
}not used anymore. Because there are other parts of the software coming
}from other developers who can read and write files at any time I am
}not able to close a descriptor on suspect. The problem is critical
}because I loose about 1000 descriptors per day and UNIX has only about
}4000 descriptors at most in stock for a process. This resolves in a
}crash every 4 days and the program is intended to run over weeks.

   You are correct, there is a descriptor leak in this case.
   Here's a 'trace' of a program calling just rexec():

stat ("/etc/svc.conf", 0x11fffd580) = 0
