I have a DEC VRT19-DA (Sony GDM-1960); it's a 19" trinitron workstation
monitor capable of 1280x1024.

It is in Austin, TX (on the UT campus).  I am having my friend, Aaron
Poser, handle the sale.  You can reach him at 512-495-3376.  You can email
him at freak4@mail.utexas.edu  The buyer who offers the highest amount or
gets there first, wins.

Questions Answered (please read):
=========================================================================
Q:  I have Linux/FreeBSD and I want to know if it will work.
A:  I have the XFREE86 modelines to make it work.  You need to be sure
that you can provide a sync-on-green signal to it.  If your video card
does not support this feature (Matrox Millenniums do), then you can build
and adaptor.  Also, you need to be sure that your video card can put out a
high enough dotclock to make it work.  It uses a 120Mhz dot clock.

Q:  I have a Windows95/3.1 machine and I want to know if it will work.
A:  It will work only if you have a fixed frequency video card.  If you
don't know whether or not you have one, you don't.

Q:  I have a windowsNT machine and I want to know if it will work.
A:  I've heard that there's a way to make fixed frequency monitors work
with NT.  However, I don't know what it is or where to find out.  Please
don't email me asking about it.

Q:  I have a macintosh and I want to know if it will work.
A:  It will work only if you have a fixed frequency video card.  If you
don't know whether or not you have one, you don't.

Q:  Do you have that modeline handy?
A:  Modeline  "1280x1024" 120.05 1280 1304 1448 1664 1024 1032 1038 1074
-hsync -vsync

Q:  Do you have instructions to make an adaptor?
A:  http://sdss.ucsd.edu/~jesus/ibm.html      I have no idea if this
circuit works for certain.  I've heard that it does.

Q:  Will you ship it?  I live in XYZ
A:  There is only one way that I will ship it.  I will ship it UPS
(through mail boxes, etc) and have them pack it up (this costs extra).
Then, I will ship it COD.  You will pay shipping.  The monitor by itself
weighs 92lbs.  It is in Austin, TX.  Don't ask me what shipping is.
Figure it out yourself.

Q:  What are some specifications of the monitor?
A:  It does 1280x1024 at 70.7 Khz horizontal and 66.5Hz vertical (give or
take a few).  Other than that, call Digital.

Q:  I want to buy it, but I want to see it work first.
A:  That is fine.  Bring a DECstation or a PC that can drive it properly.



