OK, I should have said that I believe gcc to be relatively better on the
32-visible-registers architectures than on x86, but this relative
difference is probably in the 5-20% range, depending upon the app,
instead of a factor of 2X.

Getting good performance on Pentium/PPro machines often forces you to
move from and all-inclusive big loop, using lots of temporary register
results, to a model where you break the algorithm into smaller steps
(which will fit in the woefully inadequate 6-7 regs), and then use a
block of the really fast L1 cache to store the intermediate results.

> scheduler. The OOO features of 21264, and the availability of egcs
> should improve the situation now.

Actually, from everything I've seen on my work on Pentium vs PentiumPro
handwritten asm code, having OOO features tend to help compilers a lot
more than it helps asm coders, specifically because it enables the cpu
to 'coast past' any pipeline bubbles introduced by less-than-optimal
code generation.

> "Late answers are wrong answers!"

Definitely right. :-)

