I see these threads about how Digital *could* have made it big, and how
its hardware and software were (and still are in many aspects) better.

I see some anger at the inability (or perhaps planned unwillingness) of
DEC's management to make Digital succeed.


However, since Palmer has, for all practical purposes, admitted that he
has been planning this Compaq buyout for 3 years, I am no longer so
angry at Digital. Its failures and downsizing were wanted/planned to
bring DEC down to a size where Palmer's pet project would succeed (get
bought by Compaq). So it wasn't the apparent sheer stupidity, it was all
planned. I may disagree with that plan, but it is done, and considering
the desired outcome, I have to reluctantly admit that Palmer was clever.

Yes, it is very sad to think of what Digital COULD have been. But once
reality sinks in and you swallow it, it is much better. DIGITAL IS NO
LONGER. PERIOD. 

The VMS and ALPHA balls are now in Compaq's court and it is Compaq which
will decide on their fate. I have no worries that support will continue
for years to come. I have no worries that VMS developpement will
continue for a couple more versions (to include and debug all the fancy
stuff such as Galaxies and perhaps even make Spiralog standard).

Whether Digital will continue to exist past 2-3 years, I don't know. My
gut feeling is that we are likely to see "Compaq" on what used to be DEC
products. I most certaintly expect Compaq to put its name on the
services and support areas.

The Digital we know, the Digital we were/are proud to own, the Digital
we were first to defend in religious wars (VMS vs UNIX for instance) is
no longer. It is pointless to continue to defend Digital or hope it can
fix its business. Compaq has a "commodity" mentality. If it succeeds in
integrating Digital's bits and pieces, it will mean that products
formerly done by Digital will now be commodity items. Is there a need
for a community for commodity items ?


I think that there was a very strong sense of community within the
Digital culture (both employees and customers in the same community).
DECUS was a good example of this community and a very good gauge of
customer loyalty. The amount of bitching about poor Digital business
practices was due to our extreme loyalty and our wish to help/see
Digital succeed because we know it had the potential to succeed.

Now that Digital is to be controlled by an outsider with a totally
different culture, I see the sense of community fade away, unless 
Compaq takes steps to bring gain our confidence that under Compaq 
management, the products and services we were so proud to use will
continue to make us want to defend them religiously against products
of other companies.

Under Palmer, I was forcibly detached from Digital when, being too
small, I was no longer allowed to call Digital sales folks or even DEC
Direct. So to me, Palmer was also a bit of an outsider who broke many of
the links I had with the Digital community. I used to know a lot of the
folks at the local DEC office and knew their systems enough that I
sometimes got calls from employees for help !. Now, for all I know, DEC
may have closed its office here. That is how out of touch I am.



I have confidence that Compaq has a fairly clear idea of what it indends
to do with Digital. Remember that Palmer has been courting Compaq for
some years now, and I strongly suspect that it was Compaq that told
Palmer what bits of Digital compaq was not interested in.

The way I see it, Palmer is closing up the shop and selling what is left
to Compaq. Compaq intends to slowly rebuild from the bits of Digital it
has acquired to integrate this into its own product lines. While this
process may be evolutionary, once you accept that it 
will happen, and that it is totally under the control of an unknown
entity called Compaq, it is pointless to worry. Just sit back, relax and
watch the show.

I find it ironic that in Compaq's press releases, if you read between
the lines, the strong customer loyalty was something which Compaq saw as
an asset. But this buyout, combined with the planned sabotage of Digital
by Palmer, may really hurt that loyalty, unless Compaq takes an active
role with customers to make them feel comfortable in this new home and
give us a sense that there is some growth in the future.

When Apple bought out Power Computing, Steve Job's office sent a letter
to all Power Computing customers (even if you were not under any support
contracts) welcoming us "back" to the Apple family and telling us not to
worry about our machines and that support would be continued etc.

I would expect no less from Compaq's president.



