"Len Segal" <msn_news@ne_ws.oom> writes:

>There is and has been no security in Digital for a number of years. Most are
>just taking it "one day at a time". It is inevitable that there will be
>massive layoffs once the deal is complete. Thus, many are sharpening their
>resumes at this time.

I was at DEC from 1974-1978 (if you read my account of the Blizzard of
'78 on ne.weather it's pretty obvious where I worked).

After stints as an employee, independent consultant, a startup founder, I
rejoined DEC in 1992 after Alliant entered chapter 11.  I quickly
realized I now had my most secure job since I left DEC in 1978!
While many people whine about how much DEC has changed over the years,
I saw first hand how much the industry has changed.  At Alliant the decline
in VMS was apparent years before I saw any sign DEC realized that.  When
the handwriting was on the wall for Alliant, I agonized about going
back to DEC, figuring they were still wrapped up in themselves.  Hell,
I never saw USENET postings from people at DEC.  That's improving, but
I still don't see enough.  (A lot of that's because we're too busy!)

>BTW, there are NO "DEC employees" in 1998, there are only "Digital
>employees" now.

You missed the memo - DEC has been officially reblessed as acceptable
nickname.  And some of us refused to be "Digital" employees.

 Seriously, the company is VERY different from the one I
>worked at, with much more formality.

Oh, I don't know - the dotted lines are gone from the org charts, but I
can talk to essentially anyone in the company - and do, just like I did
before.  Even as a contractor assigned to prepare NFS for OSF/1's introduction
I managed to get paid for porting tcpdump and adding NFS V3 decoding.  As
an employee I told my management I was going to be the DEC presence on
comp.protocols.nfs.  While we make better plans for the future, individuals
can still do the right thing and get some recognition for it.  With the web
and internal AltaVista database many more people can reach my ancillary work
than before.

 Ken ran a very different business where
>the employees felt as if we were all "family", Digital is now "just another
>employer".

And if Ken has stayed on for another 12? maybe only 6 months a lot of
people could have seen Chapter 11.  In the early 90s it would not have
been pretty.  Ken was okay in the 60s and 70s, but inventing DECnet
instead of using ARPAnet (blaim me for some of that), blowing the PC
market, putting up with the VMS/Ultrix acrimony, and being more
concerned about internal competition to keep prices and margins up all
were major, major mistakes.

Family works in a small company like Alliant with a small market and
small development group.  Harder to do in a big company with global
market and global competition.  Especially when it's not looking
beyond its installed base and seeing the upstarts like Sun, SGI, and
even failed companies like Alliant or Convex selling to new companies
not bound to VMS.

DEC has changed, not quite enough, but at least there will be a DEC in the
21st century.  I wasn't sure about that in 1988.

One thing is certain:  The above is _my_ view, spoken for myself.  Official
spokemen would phrase the above somewhat differently.

	-Ric
