In article <34DA9AF0.1508@EasyInternet.net>,
Paul DeMone  <PaulDeMone@EasyInternet.net> wrote:
>   When the 386 came out there was virtually no 32 bit code and people effectively
>   used it as a very fast 8088. If Intel repeats this mistake (unlikely IMHO) and
>   there is little available IA-64 native programs and people buying Merced systems
>   end up running x86 apps on an x86 operating system, then there will likely be a
>   lot more than two or three other chips beating it in performance.  However I
>   understand that some ISVs have had IA-64 compilers, simulators, and profilers
>   for quite a while so there should be some base level of native code available at
>   Merced-I's launch.

It wasn't until Windows-95 that we had something that pretended to be a 
32-bit OS as that "standard" OS from microsoft (NT was available earlier,
granted.  But how many people used it for a desktop OS before '95?).
The 386 was introduced in '86- so there was almost a decade's worth of
delay.  I'm not sure this will matter to the commercial success of Merced-
people will see the specmarks on the new chip (obtained running HP-UX or
Solaris), beleive that it is faster, and go out and spend extra money
to buy it, and never compare the speeds to the older x86 chips.  Especially
if we get Ziff-Davis loudly proclaiming that the x86 is dead...

But then again, you guys are talking about performance, and I'm talking
about marketing.  Two different subjects.


I don't speak for Bit 3.
Brian




