  I don't doubt that you've heard that.  People have been spreading 
that kind of FUD for so long I usually don't respond to it anymore.

  Linux had its stability problems back in the v0.95 days, when I 
normally experienced uptimes of only a few weeks.  It's come a long
ways since then.  My v1.2.13 systems have uptimes of >100 days, and
they're only that short because of intentional power-downs for hard-
ware upgrades and accidental power outages.  There are newer versions
which are reportedly even more stable, though I honestly don't know 
how you can get more stable than "never crashed".  Presumably they've
targeted specific known problems in oddball hardware drivers or the
like.  There are many linux systems being used as web and ftp servers
which see constant use, and I've only heard good things said about 
their reliability, when I've heard anything at all.  That's saying 
quite a lot, given that human nature is to clam up when things are 
going well and gripe when things are going badly.

  I'm not a linux fanatic; FreeBSD is at least as stable and has some
features which are implemented better or are missing in linux, and I
can happily direct people towards FreeBSD when they ask me which free
unix they should use (this happens rather often nowadays; I usually 
ask them what their friends use, and tell them to go with that.  The
differences are mostly only important to kernel hackers and fanatics,
and to the average user it's more important to have a more-experienced
user readily on-hand to help them out when they need it).  I've mostly
stuck with linux (and v1.2.13 at that) because I've hacked in some
nonstandard kernel features (which haven't diminished its stability)
and I don't see the need to learn a whole new kernel when the one I
have suffices.  I've been meaning to acquaint myself with the new v2 
kernel, but I haven't had the time. 

  I've been fairly pleased with the Alpha port of Linux, too.  I've 
heard of some graphics driver problems, but not run into them myself
(I use the RedHat distribution on a UDB).  I have access to a 533MHz
21164PC system running Linux in the office, and I've been impressed
by its power.  I doubt I'll ever buy an Intel box again.  All of my
home systems run some flavor of Unix, so there's no reason at all 
for me to buy an Intel machine when there are Alpha systems to be 
had which are more powerful and less expensive.  Only people who are
trapped into running Windows applications which haven't been ported
away from the x86 need to do that anymore.

  -- Bill Moyer

-- 




