Anil Thomas Maliyekkel wrote:
[snip]
> Aside from that I find it some what implausible that Digital improved
> integer performance over three times on a per clock basis.  That
> would imply that the 21264 is far more brainy than we've been led
> to believe.

   A three to one improvement over the 21164 is not implausible.  The
   21164 is an in-order machine with two integer units and rather rigid
   issue rules.  When a cache read miss occurs it locks up tight for
   the duration of the latency.

   On the other hand the 21264 has four integer execution units and by
   far the most extensive out of order execution capability seen on a
   processor so far.  It can have up 80 instructions in flight at a given
   time.  During cache read misses it can speculatively execute past the
   offending load instruction and perform useful work.  Finally, for
   deeply pipelined superscalar processors up to 20-30% of the potential
   performance is lost due to incorrectly predicted branches; the 21264
   has a much more sophisticated branch prediction system than the 21164.

All opinions strictly my own.

--
Paul W. DeMone                 The 801 experiment SPARCed an ARMs race
Kanata, Ontario                to put more PRECISION and POWER into
demone@mosaid.com              architectures with MIPSed results but
PaulDeMone@EasyInternet.net    ALPHA's well that ends well.




