Paul DeMone wrote in message <34D02C2C.131E@EasyInternet.net>...

>    I don't see any technical reasons to believe such grandiose predictions
of
>    Alpha superiority over Merced/IA-64.  The clock rate crank becomes ever
so
>    harder to turn as 1 GHz is crossed.  Also, the potentially simpler
dispatch
>    logic of Merced CPUs due to its static scheduling approach will likely
reduce
>    or even eliminate the clock rate advantages Alpha has historically
enjoyed.
>    Similarly, attempts to build very wide issue machines will likely play
to IA-64's
>    strengths as the dependency checking logic and out-of-order support
mechanisms
>    needed for dynamically scheduled CPUs like the 21264 experience
exponential
>    complexity increases with issue width.


The dependency checking isn't a bottleneck in Alpha because of the
regularity of the
instruction format which allows this to be determined in parallel with
decode.  IA-64
still has to do the dependency checking (based on the articles I've read
about IA-64)
The potential advantage to IA-64 is that the compiler has already done the
register
renaming that the 21264 has to do dynamically.  On the other hand, for a
given I-cache
and I-fetch bandwidth, Alpha has access to 30% more instructions than IA-64.

However, the challenge in getting Alpha performance up is to get the
compilers to
generate the right sequence of instructions to get the data loaded in time
to keep the
execution units busy.  To do this scheduling, the compiler has replicate the
dependency
calculations and apply algorithms to reorder or modify the I stream to keep
things busy.

Twenty five years ago, the hardware and compiler people talked about
architecture and
decided that the VAX was the way to go.  After about five years, the
hardware and
compiler groups had agreed that this previous agreement was wrong and the
VAX
architecture was subsetted by ripping out over 60% of the complexity and by
defining
rules by which the compilers would effectively further subset the
architecture.  The
hardware either trapped to the OS on a large number of little used
instructions or
it dropped into sort of a hardware emulation mode if the rules weren't
observed.

Even with Alpha, it took several years for the hardware and software people
to agree
that there were some significantly incorrect assumptions and that the ISA
had to be
modified.

Intel hasn't proved itself to be any better than DEC with the Intel
architecture.  Consider
the MMX instructions; my perception is that there are going to be about a
half dozen
variations with Intel contributing at least 2 and possibly 3 or more
variations.  Clearly,
if a mistake has been made in the ISA, you should correct it as quickly as
possible.
But the number of changes in the architecture needs to be very limited or
else you
bear an immense burden long in the future.

So, until there is real world experience with IA-64, the concepts on which
IA-64
are based are an unknown.  It might be that the inevitable compromise is
ideal
or it might be flawed so that a second generation is required to exploit the
potential, or it might be flawed as severely as the VAX was.   (There was a
very big gap between the VAX-11/780 and the MicroVAX II which is what
kept the VAX commercially viable for 20 years.)

>    Regardless of the technical issues of Merced vs Alpha is the business
aspect.
>    Each new generation of CPU is ever more expensive to conceptualize,
design,
>    implement, and verify.  Is the Alpha development process so "lean and
mean"
>    that it can continue on the technology treadmill given its rather
mediocre
>    commercial success so far?


One advantage that Alpha had that Intel won't have is that it was quite
difficult to
do an apples to apples comparison of Alpha versus VAX performance.  The VAX
performance was measured in VUPs which were the basis of the SPECmark
but over the years prior to Alpha they had diverged.  Dollar for dollar,
Alpha
offered little to recommend it over VAX/VMS on day one other than a path
beyond the limitations of a 32 bit architecture for a single OS.  On the
other hand,
DEC OSF/1 and Alpha were able to exceed the performance of other unix
offerings which might have been due as much to a better chip design and
technology
rather than the Alpha RISC architecture; there was no DEC OSF/1 running on
NVAX.

When IA-64 comes out, the first thing that will happen is that everyone and
their
brother will be booting unix and nt on IA-64, Alpha, Pentium II and probably
Pentium III
and Pentium IV in slot 1, 2, 3 and 4, or whatever the marketing people
decide to name
them, along with the Winchip 2 and 3, the AMD 786, and Cyrix ??? and my
guess is
that the initial IA-64 systems will be beaten by at least two chips other
than Alpha.
Intel won't be able to afford to hold back the performance of the Pentium
without
giving a boost to Intel's competitors.






