There are two EE Times articles on the Alpha that I could find
from the past week:

The lastest one:

http://techweb.cmp.com/eet/news/98/992news/with.html
"With_Intel's_help,_Alpha_21264_will_hit_1-GHz" (6:00 p.m. EST, 2/2/98)

says:

   Harry Copperman, senior vice president of Digital's products division,
   said the latest Alpha processor, named the 21264, was being introduced
   with a clock frequency of 600-MHz, and that the frequency is expected
   to be increased to 1-GHz by the year 2000. The chip yields 40-SPECmark
   integer performance and 60-SPECmark floating-point performance at
   600-MHz, and will rise to 100-SPECmark integer performance and
   150-SPECmark floating-point performance at 1 GHz, Copperman said.
   "This is two times the performance of any other architecture available
   or on the horizon," he said. The Alpha 21264 features twice the clock
   frequency and four times the performance of Digital's previous
   generation Alpha 21164 processor.


The other one is at:

http://techweb.cmp.com/eet/news/98/992news/digital.html
"Digital_attacks,_Compaq_adopts_Intel's_Merced" (11:45 p.m. EST, 1/30/98)

and says:

   Digital (Maynard, Mass.) is expected to soon announce availability of
   its next-generation 0.35-micron 21264 processors running at 400 to 433
   MHz, a device the company claims will provide twice the performance as
   the current 21164. To get such a performance boost, the chip will use
   out-of-order execution and double the size of the cache memory, with a
   bandwidth rating of 8 Gbytes per second. The L2 cache will run as fast
   as 5 Gbytes/s, according to Y.J. Kim, associate director of Alpha
   processor marketing at Samsung Semiconductor, here.


Of course the first article has an explicit statement of performance
at 600MHz of ~40 SPECInt95 by a Digital exec, but the other one talks 
about sampling at 400 to 433 MHz and performing twice as well as the
21164.  This isn't a clear statement though, since they could be
talking about twice the performance at a given clock speed which,
given that the Alpha 21164 at 600MHz has SPECInt95 ratings of 18
and SPECfp95 of 28, would be consistent with statement of the
Digital exec.  Though I don't quite get the last sentence from
that first excerpt regarding twice the clock frequency and four
times the performance.  Twice the frequency is two high
unless they plan on getting to around 1200 to 1400MHz, and four times
is to little if they plan on getting to 100 SPECint95 and 150 SPECfp95.

Regarding out of order execution, I would think the 21264 needs
aggressive scheduling and completion hardware to make effective use of
those two new integer units.  



