In article <Pine.SGI.3.95L.980128115059.871A-100000@tower.york.ac.uk>, Paul
Vetch <phv100@york.ac.uk> wrote:

Hi Paul,

Don't know if you've gotten any other answers, so I'll comment on what I know...

> I'm considering running an Alpha system with NT to replace my current dual
> PII setup which is inherently unstable.
...
> What are the DEC motherboards like...any good?  What do they have
> on-board - SCSI controller by any chance?

In general, we've had good luck with the Alphas.  Only a power supply
problem on our first one, but no problems on the next 6 that we've worked
with.

Not sure about on-board SCSI controllers.  There are many flavors of Alphas
(altho the Personal Alphas seem to be the most popular now), so it would be
best to inquire about this from whomever configures/sells the system you
plan to buy.  On our original (pre-Personal) Alpha, we have a 2nd SCSI bus
(for external devices) that is not a part of the motherboard (I think).

> What are the cooling issues for Alpha CPUs...do they need dedicated
> cooling/heat pipes etc or is a big heatsink and good airflow sufficient?

No problem here - nothing fancy is needed: good airflow in a fairly
well-cooled/ventilated room will be fine.  Our original Alpha has two sets
of cooling fans: one that runs all the time and a second set that kicks-in
if needed (and we've never had them kick-in).  The Personal Alphas are
smaller and also have no special needs.

Hope this helps!

Peyton

