As an ex-DECcie who is married to a current Digital employee . . .

There is and has been no security in Digital for a number of years. Most are
just taking it "one day at a time". It is inevitable that there will be
massive layoffs once the deal is complete. Thus, many are sharpening their
resumes at this time.

Speaking strictly for myself, I think that Compaq will trim the company
drastically and move the majority of what is left to the South (TX in
particular), keeping many small offices (in scattered locations) for support
services and sales. As a stockholder in Digital, I finally look forward to
getting more value for the stock than I paid for it over the 13 years I was
with DEC.

A serious advantage to Compaq and perhaps a detriment to the ingrained
Digital management is the fact that John Rose is likely to be greatly
involved in the transition. John was once my boss's boss at DEC and was the
VP of the PC group at DEC when he left for Compaq. John is now Sr. VP of
Enterprise Computing at Compaq (although one news story reported him as CFO)
and is likely to know where many of the skeletons are in Digital's closet!

BTW, there are NO "DEC employees" in 1998, there are only "Digital
employees" now. Seriously, the company is VERY different from the one I
worked at, with much more formality. Ken ran a very different business where
the employees felt as if we were all "family", Digital is now "just another
employer".

