In article <blake-0302982354110001@rno-max4-29.gbis.net>, blake@squonk.reno.nv.us (Blake Dollens) writes:
:We have a bunch of DEC LK-250 keyboards we'd like to use. Does anyone know
:of an adapter to change the propietary LK-250 plug into a PS/2 one? Where
:can we get some?

  The LK250 was often shipped with the then-current IBM standard PC/XT/AT
  keyboard plug, or with adapters.  (The LK250 was originally shipped with
  the VAXmate, an XT/AT-class PC system.)

  The IBM PS/2 system was the first of a new series, and used a different
  keyboard plug.

:Barring an adapter, does anyone know the pinouts of the LK-250 plug so I
:can splice on a PS/2 connector?

  Several vendors make/made adapters to convert PC/XT/AT keyboards for use
  on the then-new PS/2 systems.  I do not know if these adapters are still
  available, nor if they will work in any particular situation...

  I would recommend acquiring or creating an adapter cable, I would *not*
  recommend cutting the existing cabling.  (If the attached pinout turns
  out to be incorrect, or if the adapter is created incorrectly, you are
  otherwise then left with a non-functional keyboard...)

  The adapter wiring should look something like this:

    LK250                PS2 DIN
    -----                -------
    1: shield ground --- 3: protective ground
    2: keyboard clock -- 2: keyboard clock
    3: keyboard data --- 1: keyboard data
    4: +5V ------------- 9: +5V
    5: Signal ground --- 4: signal ground
    6: disconnected      5: not used

  Leave the orange wire in the LK250 cable disconnected...

  Experiment *before* cutting the wires.

 -------------------------- pure personal opinion ---------------------------
 Hoff (Stephen) Hoffman   OpenVMS Engineering   hoffman@xdelta.ZZenet.dec.com
