In article <upidStmM9GA.259@upnetnews02.moswest.msn.net>,
Len Segal <msn_news@ne_ws.oom> wrote:
>As an ex-DECcie who is married to a current Digital employee . . .
>
>There is and has been no security in Digital for a number of years. Most are
>just taking it "one day at a time". It is inevitable that there will be
>massive layoffs once the deal is complete. Thus, many are sharpening their
>resumes at this time.
>
>BTW, there are NO "DEC employees" in 1998, there are only "Digital
>employees" now. Seriously, the company is VERY different from the one I
>worked at, with much more formality. Ken ran a very different business where
>the employees felt as if we were all "family", Digital is now "just another
>employer".
>
>Regards,
>Len Segal
>Segal Computer Consulting
>Microsoft - MVP (DTS), ClubWin
>CompuServe - CSP

Len -- ain't it the truth.  I left in 1986 after 5 years and missed DEC and 
tried to stay in touch with the company (I still have the stock...and some 
old PDT11/150 floppies).

I've been at IBM, Concurrent Computer (before and after the Masscomp merger)
Alliant and Pyramid Technology, and now at Lucent... I've never felt as
close to a company as I did at DEC.  We were family with a mission of 
pushing interactive coputing.

I always felt what hurt DEC was not putting a Jack Shields or someone from
service in charge of the operation.  The service folk lived, ate and
slept with the customers... 

The folks who priced VMS out of the market... the folks that began treating
service like revenue was more important than customer retention... the folks
that didn't see the PC and workstations sliding into DEC strongholds...
the folks that didn't want to use the "Unix" word... blew it.

DEC put Vaxes on HSC's for AT&T (but did it quietly for a customer).
DEC didn't consider that there was a need for commercial Unix support on
Vaxes until it was very late in the game.

I often wonder if the rumored AT&T-DEC merger of 1984 (and AT&T's plan to
contract Unix admin out at Bell Labs to DEC which died around the same time)
was often part of the problem.  (This info seems to me the X-Files of the
DEC history.  My wife was at AT&T and also remembers the merger rumors).

Yes I know about the oursource deal 'cause I was approached to take a job
with DEC as an Operator or Sysadmin leaving DEC Field Service to do it.
It fell through around 1984. (No one seems to admit to this...)

I think the loss of the merger pushed AT&T hard with the 3b2 commercialization
and the NCR take over.

(now that I'm at Lucent after 10 years in Unix Sysadmin I'm wondering if it
was an omen somehow)

Bill
ex-DEC Field Service (and still VERY proud of it)
