Alan Winston - SSRL Admin Cmptg Mgr (winston@SSRL.SLAC.STANFORD.EDU) wrote:
: I'll now prove that _I'm_ a complete ninny about chip architecture, who
: wouldn't even be reading this thread if it weren't cross-posted to
: comp.sys.dec: Doesn't a die shrink mean that everything on the chip is
: closer together, so signals have less distance to travel and thus arrive
: sooner, thus increasing speed?

Pretty much by definition this holds for non-self-timed circuits. (A self
timed circuit is designed to run as fast as it can and to automatically
synchronize all inputs and outputs as they occur.) Some modern micros
include small bits of self-timed logic, but generally the overall design is
synchronous or clocked. So yes, the speed of "everything" *on the chip*
goes up linearly with clockspeed.

This does not result in a liner speedup of application performance with
clockspeed since lots of the whole computer is not "on the chip." E.g.
cache, memory, disks don't necessarily get any faster when CPU clockspeed
increases.

But all of this is moot for the original argument. The issue is that it
appears the marketing claims are for a non-linear (greater than of course)
scaling of SPEC performance with clockspeed. This really doesn't make sense
if its just a die shringk and clockspeed increase. (Well, it can happen in
really localized portions of the clockspeed range due to bus clocks being
related to CPU clocks by integral factors. Raising the CPU clock slightly
might allow one to hop to the next bus or cache clock "quantum".)

-Z-



