>    John Carr (jfc@mit.edu)

Actually, the surrounding chipset 'Tsunami' support's three types of DRAM:

    SDRAM
    DDR-SDRAM(Dual-Data-Rate SDRAM)
    DDR LW ESDRAM(Dual-Data-Rate Late-Write ESDRAM)

100Mhz SDRAM shipping now, will give a 1.6GBps peak throughput
and ~740MBps sustained, on a 21264/Tsunami system.
The DDR-SDRAM(from Samsung) more or less capeable doubling
the throughput, will proberbly be shipping and used on the first 21264
systems introduced from Samsung.
The DDR-LW-ESDRAM running at 200MHz should give a performance
boost up 4,3GBps, but will not be available untill early/mid 99.

The same goes for the Cache-RAM to be in the 21264/Tsunami setup.
Here are the three types supported:

    SSRAM max. throughput at 64bit bus width ~ 2.1GBps(now).
    BSSRAM max throughput at 64bit bus width ~4.3GBps(now)
    FSRAM(DD LW FSRAM) max est. throughput at 64bit bus width ~4.8GBps

The FSRAM is being developed by Motorola in the moment, and will proberbly
also hit the street early 99.

Now making a system capeable of supporting future RAM types, I would say
it's
fair to expect the performance/MHz to change a bit.

Regards, Anders Blicher Petersen







