In article <34D5BF03.44090837@devbn0.triumf.ca>, "Frederick W. Bach" <bach@devbn0.triumf.ca> writes:
>
>   That's not what I heard.  I heard that the .exe's are incompatible.
>   Will an .exe file, compiled on an Intel Pentium running NT, run on
>   an Alpha running NT?  Or will the program need recompiling and
>   relinking on the Alpha?  Will there now have to be an Intel version, =
>   a Mac version and an Alpha version of the .exe file?

Correct.  Digital has FX!32, which interprets Intel .exe files on
Alpha, and it will be part of the Windows NT kernel for Alpha.  When
made part of the kernel, it allegedly will run any Intel .exe file. 
One might imagine by the time Merced is an actual product, similar
technology will be available for Merced to run x86 .exe files.

There is always room for improvement in emulation technology, but
recompiling and relinking a native image will always be faster.  Java
byte code, on the other hand, is machine independent.  It's slower than
genuine compiled images, but a fast Alpha or Pentium II minus the Java
interpreter overhead ought to be pretty impressive.

A recent issue of Byte had an interesting article on Merced, which
moves a good deal of RISC chips' optimization into the compilers,
enough that when Merced II comes out, it will need different compiler
optimizations from the original Merced.  I'm not going to start
worrying about it now, but it will be a challenge a few years ahead.

