peterm@tesla.EECS.Berkeley.EDU (Peter Mardahl) writes:

>Does anyone know when actual systems based on the 21264 are to be available?

>DEC's been hyping it for a long time now, and the hype has worked, I'd
>like to buy one if I can afford it.

>IBM's Power3 is supposed to get comparable performance to the 21264, using
>their new .25u copper-metallization process.  When are machines with the
>Power3 to be available?


According to those press releases from today, "summer 1998".  Same time
for POWER3.  POWER3 is supposed to do 70 SPECfp, if they meet that, it'll
have the 21264 beat out of the gate for floating point, though it is very
likely it'll lag badly in SPECint (I guess IBM figures you just don't buy
the POWER systems if you think integer performance is even remotely as
important as fp)  It will be interesting to see if 21264 systems can steal
the STREAM crown from IBM, which have held it since the introduction of
the POWER systems, IIRC.

HP will be shipping PA-8500 systems this summer as well, which will likely
lag the 21264 somewhat, doing maybe 25-30 SPECint and 35-40 SPECfp (these
are my guesses based on the announced 360 MHz initial clock for the 8500)
Since it is based in .25 micron, and they managed 236 MHz on the 8200 at
.5 micron, they should be able to at a minimum top 500 MHz with the
8500, to keep the 21264 from being able to widen its lead as its clock
ramps up.  HP will need to update their runway bus, which maxes at 960MB/s
(and doesn't come close to that for a single CPU anyway) at some point,
and move from EDO to SDRAM (or maybe RDRAM, given their close relationship
with Intel working on Merced) to keep the CPU fed as it clocks up.

SGI recently announced 250MHz R10K systems, but those won't even be in the
same league.  Maybe R12K, but I don't know if anything has been released
about those or when they'll appear.  Ditto Sun, IMHO, despite their talk
about the UltraSparc III (they have talked about performance and failed to
deliver for over five years now, so I won't believe anything they say unless
they prove it with actual shipping hardware.

At a minimum, the POWER3 and PA-8500 will make laughable Digital's claims
of "two to five times the performance of any architecture available now and
in the future".  Digital may do well to even have performance leadership in
SPECfp.  They like to conveniently forget the nearly half year HP held that
lead in '96 and month or two in '97 (with shipping systems, not preannounced
numbers)  All this even if Merced never sees the light of day.

Not to take away from the 21264, it is very impressive to be talking of 1GHz
clock rates in a "brainiac" out of order CPU.

-- 
Douglas Siebert                Director of Computing Facilities
douglas-siebert@uiowa.edu      Division of Mathematical Sciences, U of Iowa

If you let the system beat you long enough, eventually it'll get tired.



