> To quote the press release at http://www.digital.com/flash/f195/
> 
> "The third-generation Alpha 21264 family will deliver up to five times the
> highest performance of any architecture available now and in the near
> future. Performance will begin at an estimated 40 SPECint95 and 60 SPECfp95
> and will reach more than 100 SPECint95 and 150 SPECfp95, and operate at more
> than 1000 MHz by the year 2000."
> 

So we have 100 SPECint95 at 1000Mhz, and 40 SPECint95's initially.

Now, the best case assuming no redesign is that SPECint is proprtional
to clock rate, ie 

SPECINT = k * CLOCKRATE IN MHZ, 

but more realistically you'll get some levelling off at high clock rates,
due to higher cache miss penalties

So from the predication k is about 0.1, at one Ghz, probably more at
a lower clock rate. But let's be optimistic and say that its exactly
0.1.

Now, we get 40 SPECInts at release, so the clock rate should be 
40 / k = 400Mhz. If at introduction the clock rate is _more_ than this then
they're not going to get 100 SPECInts at 1Ghz.

Remember this is being optimistic, being realistic k will be more
than 0.1 at low ClockRates, so the clock rate would have to be less
than 40 / k if they want to make their prediction.

Basically the point I'm making is that either

1) I've missed something obvious

or
 
2) If the 21264 debuts at _more_ than 400Mhz, then it won't 
achieve 100 SPECInt95 at 1Ghz. This prediction looks like
hype. ( NB I'm not saying I won't buy one anyway ). If they
debut at 600Mhz / 40 SPECInt[1], then the best they can do is
66.6 SPECInt95 at 1GHz.

Tom.

I've set the follow-ups to only comp.arch, because I want
to see if my assumptions are correct. 

