In article <34D79952.CB2EB9FE@nastything.com>, Cyber Sidhe <cyber-sidhe@nastything.com> writes:
:I am buying a DECstation/unix workstation 5000/200 and was wondering if
:anybody new where I can find out any info on the internet about these?

  If you are asking these questions, I'd recommend looking for
  something newer.  Such as an Alpha, or an Intel-based PC.  For
  information on the DECstation 5000 series, I'd try one of the
  various available search engines...

:I have tried digital's home site, but to no avail.

  Only current/recent systems are typically listed.

:My questions are:  How easy are they to upgrade?

  No more difficult than any other system of a similar vintage.

:find parts for? 

  No more difficult than any other system of a similar vintage.
  (These systems predate the commodity PC hardware and standards
  that most folks will be familiar with and will want to use...)

  Video uses the synch-on-green three-BNC system that was common
  on workstations of the time -- this system is incompatible with
  the five-BNC graphics controllers in common use now.

  For information on synch-on-green, visit http://www.dejanews.com/
  and look for my previous responses on this topic...  Usually, the
  video question arises in the context of "can I use an old DIGITAL
  VRxxx monitor on my PC?", and the answer is usually "no".

:are they still being made?

  These systems were discontinued many years ago -- these systems
  were commonly sold roughly eight years ago.

:and what are your personal experiences with them? 

  They were nice systems for their time.  They were rather faster
  than most of the VAX systems of the time.  Now, they're just slow.

  Packaged systems had SCSI, keyboard, mouse, ThinWire Ethernet,
  and an ULTRIX license.  MIPS R3000 chipset at 25 MHz.  R3010 FPU.
  64K write-through D-cache, 64K I-cache.  Memory 8MB up to 120MB.
  There were a number of variants of the 200 series, typically with
  different graphics controllers and/or other variations...

  ULTRIX.  No Windows NT support.  You might be able to find Linux
  for this platform.

 -------------------------- pure personal opinion ---------------------------
 Hoff (Stephen) Hoffman   OpenVMS Engineering   hoffman@xdelta.ZZenet.dec.com
