Rob Young wrote in message <1998Jan29.000909.1@eisner>...

>"The companies will cooperate on a single programming model and a single
API for
>64-bit Windows NT running on Intel and Alpha systems. This cooperation will
>provide source code compatibility between Intel and Alpha systems for
Windows
>NT application developers."


If you had followed the announcement on the Internet and heard Bill Gates in
the Q&A, then you would have heard him clearly explain that NT 5.0 is NOT
going to provide the support that the above quote refers to.

On the other hand, this does not mean that 64 bit support is not needed, and
not available, today on WinNT.

The release sometimes called NT 4.5 but more properly called something like
"enterprise server edition" provides support for 64 bit memory management.
And Oracle, for one, makes use of this capability to provide very large
memory database support on Windows NT today.  NT 5.0 will provide some
limited additional 64 bit support which will make it a bit easier to do what
Oracle is doing.

However, Windows NT is not the only OS around, just as Alpha is not the only
CPU architecture.

The agreement with Microsoft parallels the recent agreement between Sequent
and Digital for joint development of an enhanced version of Digital UNIX
that will be compiled from the same source into Alpha and IA-64 versions.
(This agreement is not exclusive; Sequent and Digital are looking for
additional partners in this agreement to ensure that there is a single unix
binary standard for IA-64 and a large application base.)

The big questions are":

   Is it safe for application developers to ignore 64 bit RISC application
support?
  Can an application developer allow Microsoft to get a head start in this
long term investment?

Bill Gates, et al, wanted Windows versions of Lotus 123 and WordPerfect
early in the life of Windows because those applications would enhance the
desirability of Windows.  However, the lack of enthusiasm for Windows by
those two companies did not deter Microsoft from developing Word, Excel,
etc. which have since become defacto standards.

More important than the question of whether Alpha has a 3 month or a 3 year
head start on IA-64 is the question of whether 64 bit applications developed
for Alpha will have a 6 month or 6 year head start over 64 bit applications
developed for IA-64.

The final question might be:
   Who do you want selling the 64 bit application systems you will be using
in five to ten years?






