Jermoe Fine wrote regarding use of old programs and symbol names ...

If there are no copyright messages in the source, then it is generally
considered OK to distribute, although if there are names, it is
generally considered good etiquette to attempt to contact the writer for
permission if possible.  If you've made a reasonable effort then as far
as I understand, it's OK.  You should also make reasonable effort to
ensure the modules aren't direct lifts out of similar software with the
copyright messages removed.

It's all a matter of reasonable efforts.  Any software I've copied like
that, I acknowledge the original writer, or try to indicate the steps
made to locate the author.

It is also a good idea to include a "please include this header when
modifying" clause so that the original source of the software remains
acknowledged.

Now as to symbol names themselves, clearly, there is no copyright on
their use ... but the real concern is that anything that maps the
monitor uses the correct name for current versions, or that you don't
accidentally doubly define a symbol.  For that a scan through the
libraries and the monitor map should suffice.

Stuart



