I thought that the following comments from x-jsue@cs.indiana.edu were
right on! I remember reading in the 80s how the Sony corporation were
increasing market share with a strategy of creating new smaller/cheaper/
better products which were intended to displace their OWN products on
the market. This not only produced very competitive products, it
encouraged 'upgrade' sales to existing customers, even though the
profit per item actually fell.

Digital on the otherhand were very protective of current products and
profits, and seemed to be going to great lengths with LMF and licensing
policies to maintain the status quo. In fact for a while there I
wondered if Digital might be spending more on legal issues than on
engineering!

In the end it seems that protection of market share is FAR more
important
than short term profits. I'm sure that this is a lesson not lost on
Apple
either - but it has allowed the introduction of clone systems far too
late!

It will be interesting to see what Compaq does with VMS now. I imagine
that they too will concentrate on simply maintaining existing customers.
Pity, if VMS had been ported to commodity priced systems and made
cheaply
available it would have had a far different future....


Paul Nankervis
Naturally all the opinions expressed here are personal.... etc


