In article <34D91A70.F1357D47@idirect.com>,
Jerome Fine  <jhfine@idirect.com> wrote:
>Some of the friends I work with are considering releasing some of their
>old RT-11 programs into the public domain.  However, they are a bit
>concerned that they copied some of the commonly used symbol names
>from DECUS programs in the past, mostly from MACRO programs.

FWIW, at least in things other than computer software there is the concept
of "fair use" of copyrighted material, where if you snip out insignificant
little pieces (such as briefly quoting another author) it's not infringement.
In any case if it's really just a couple of symbol names that are obvious
anyway (like the names for I/D PSECTS and the main entry and the stack)
then it would be hard for them to be unique enough to be copyrightable.
If they hacked out entire subroutines, well that's different.

Re lack of a copyright notice, I believe that whether that matters depends
on when it was released.  My understanding is that before some relatively
recent copyright convention (held in the 1970s I think), what counted is what
notice was on the work when it was published (and if was never published
then it may not be copyrighted), but these days a work is implicitly
copyrighted as soon as it's created, the copyright notice just underlines
that fact and registering the copyright (which is optional) decides who has
the burden of proof if you end up in court arguing about ownership.

John Wilson
D Bit



