Jerome Fine <jhfine@idirect.com> writes:

>Some of the friends I work with are considering releasing some of their
>old RT-11 programs into the public domain.  However, they are a bit
>concerned that they copied some of the commonly used symbol names
>from DECUS programs in the past, mostly from MACRO programs.
>They always assumed that there were no copy-right problems or
>restrictions with the use of these programs, but they have asked me
>to check.  They did not find any copy-right in the files, by the way, so
>there was none of the standard "Copy-right, but free to be distributed
>stuff".  The ONLY thing they are concerned about is the use of the
>symbol names which were adopted since they seemed to be in common
>use and every program that used the same value seemed to adopt that
>symbol.  I don't have any specific examples to give (obviously since this
>is what they are concerned about), but for commonly used values in an
>RT-11 program, most source code seemed to used the same symbol name.
>The code is not a problem since it was all developed in house.

The whole purpose of the standard symbol names (from someone who
was part of it all) was so that people WOULD use them... so that
programs written by one person would be understandable by another
person because of the use of the standard symbol names.  It would
also be easier to debug because it would be more easy to see if a
symbol was being used incorrectly... like if the code is getting
the date word, but the comments say something about getting the
pointer to the PNAME table.  It would also allow a global search
for all references to a specific datum, if there was a bug in
relation to use of that information.

Heck, the RT utility sources don't all use the 'standard' names
as have been defined in SYSTEM.MLB...  It was a plan of the
development team that the utilities would be fixed over time,
eventually all would be modified so that they used the standard
symbols...  Surely we attempted in the later releases (once
SYSTEM.MLB was introduced) to ensure that any new code used the
standard names.

>Incidentally, some of these DECUS source files are now 20 years old, if
>that makes a difference.

I can't see that it would, but there is a greater chance that they
don't use the names as defined in SYSTEM.MLB, since it didn't exist
that long ago...

