Rob Young wrote:
[snip]
>         But at 120 million transistors what is the PA-8500 meant for?
>         A stop-gap prior to Merced.  How would you ever make an affordable
>         workstation out of it?

    Look at what it is replacing, the PA-8000 and PA-8200.  Think of the
    expensive CPU packaging, system PWB, and boutique SRAMs needed for
    the two 128 bit wide external L1 caches running at 160 to 236 MHz that
    these CPUs require.

    The PA-8500 in 0.25 um will probably have more or less the same die size
    as the PA-8000/8200 in 0.50 um (An SRAM cell is very tiny in 0.25 um
    logic processes, between 9 and 11 square microns).  The wafer cost will be
    higher for the 0.25 um process but on the other hand the PA-8500 die area is
    roughly 3/4 SRAM in which many single point defects can be repaired through
    redundancy vs the PA-8000/8200 die which is entirely logic and vulnerable to
    even a single defect.  Finally, the PA-8500 likely requires many hundreds of
    fewer pins than its predecessors, and possibly tens of fewer watts, so it is
    likely far cheaper to package.

    It is entirely possible that the PA-8500 will be a less expensive device
    than the PA-8000/PA-8200.  Combined with the elimination of the expensive
    SRAM devices from the bill of materials, plus the potential use of cheaper
    PWB technology, the PA-8500 will likely allow both a significant reduction
    in the system manufacturing cost and healthy increase in performance for a
    relatively small amount of development effort.


[snip]

All opinions strictly my own.

