It's not often that I find myself even partially in agreement with Andrew.
But there's a first time for everything :-) I would agree that few customers
will jump immediately on NT 5.0, I don't think real live IT environments
*immediately* jump on any new OS release regardless of the vendor. That
said, where I would disagree with Andrew is on how he is characterizing NT
5.0. Yes, all this new functionality will be in NT 5.0, but a lot of is
options, for example, the base package won't have Hydra that will be
seperately purchaseable, many of the other new features are modules which
you don't have to load. The biggest migration effort will Active Directory
which you'll need, but the rest of the like Zero Admin will only need to go
on a few of your servers.

In my experience, (going back to shipping servers for NT 3.1) customers
demand that new systems come with either the old release or the new release
till about six months into the new release. After that, you couldn't give
them a new server with the old release of the OS. Given typical IT cycles
that probably means they'll be rolling NT 5.0 out big time about a year from
release which means that any theoretical advantage for 64 bit Alpha is
questionable at best.


