>    a Mac version and an Alpha version of the .exe file?

Unless you run an emulator, you will need to compile each app for each
target platform. But judging on how Microsoft was able to muscle its way
to force folks to produce apps for WIN95 (if it doesn't run on WIN-32,
it can't sport the Windows logo anymore), I think that the same will
happen with IA64.

Once MS produces a "new" version of NT which runs on 64bit chips only,
software publishers will be quick to produce a new version of their app
which also runs on that 64 bit architecture and the older 32 bit
architectures will be left behind.

And because new versions of apps will have fancy new "must have"
features, folks will go and blindly upgrade their PCs to the new 64 bit
chip to get that new function, otherwise they will look like the poor
kids on the block with old "stale" software.

Alpha could have taken off a long time ago if Digital had convinced
Adobe to produce some of the key software (Photoshop, Premi=E8re, Page
Maker) to run on Alpha NT and not on Intel 8086s. You need a "power app"
to attract folks to a =

platform.



