In article <34D6856C.79690276@cyberway.com.sg>,
tffoong  <tffoong@cyberway.com.sg> wrote:
>I am new to DEC Unix systems, and I am trying to install a new Pioneer
>24x CD-ROM drive to one of the old Alphastation 200 running DEC Unix ver
>3.2. I have some minor problems in mounting the device even though the
>systems seems to recognise the CD-ROM drive (Pioneer CD-ROm drive was
>shown when I typed in the show device command).
>
>Can anyone help me witht the correct mounting commands?

I don't know the exact problem you're having.
You say the device is recognized by the OS.  I suppose
it is a SCSI CDROM?

The 'mount' command needed actually depends on the CD's filesystem
type.

This is for CDFS:

mount -t cdfs -r /dev/cdrom /mnt
(/dev/cdrom is your cdrom device, probably rz[something]c)

The -r is for readonly.

For UFS, it might be:

mount -t ufs -r /dev/cdrom /mnt

If neither of these works, possibly it's because you don't
have CDFS compiled into your kernel.  Recompile your kernel
and make sure it supports CDFS.

If you don't know how to rebuild the kernel, start by reading
'man sysconfig' and 'man doconfig'

PeterM



