mark@cita.toronto.edu (Mark Bartelt) wrote:

>I'll limit my own comments to just one:  Digital's long-running
>antipathy to UNIX, starting at least as far back as the late 70s
>and continuing, to one degree or another, until the early 90s at
>least (and many would opine that it continues to this day, though
>I'd admit that once the Alpha+OSF1 combination was on the market,
>Digital was finally serious about providing a decent UNIX).

I come from the other side of this perspective.  If Digital hadn't let
marketroids hamper the performance of the VAX chips, and the pricing
of those systems, VMS would have been much more widespread and we
would have fewer problems in our business information systems.  The
prevalence of Unix became a necessity because the business system
vendors (e.g., IBM, DEC) chose to milk the technology by releasing, at
best, marginal performance increases (up through the 6000-400) at very
high prices.  This goes all the way down to the workstation level:  I
had a $25,000 - $50,000 (with software) VAXstation 2000 that couldn't
come very close to the Unix workstations of the day which were priced
in the $10,000-15,000 range.  And this was a pretty constant situation
throughout the late 80s and early 90s.  

I believe that DEC could have produced much faster increments in VAX
processors at a faster pace, and even at better prices.  The
incredible hype around RISC became a self-fulfilling prophecy - except
that DEC did finally take the VAX much further performance-wise than
the RISC vendors' FUD gave them (and CISC) credit for.  I think that's
a credit to the exceptional engineers at DEC, but unfortunately their
inept marketing gurus won the business decisions.  Also unfortunate is
that they continued this practice with the Alpha system business.

Hardly anything else comes close to the capabilities of DEC
hardware+VMSclusters when balanced system performance and overall
system availability are considered.  Sure, there are always certain
features that may be nice-to-haves, but overall it's been rock solid,
even with some very complex systems... something I haven't seen with
equally-complex configurations of hardware and software on most other
platforms.  The OS has the look and feel of an *architecture*
implementation rather than a hodge-podge of thrown-together pieces
with a similar name to bind them together.

Had they played their technologies better, there wouldn't have been
any *need* to move to Unix for most *businesses*.  Just the same as
there wouldn't have been any need to move to NT now.  There is nothing
inherently better in these systems to make them significantly better.

>
>It's interesting to speculate how different things might have been
>if Digital had taken a strong, pro-active approach with regard to
>UNIX support, and UNIX-based products, to serve the needs of those
>customers who ended up dumping Digital for Sun, and later for SGI,
>HP, and even -- yes even -- IBM.

I like to speculate how different things would have been if DEC had
kept their desktop-to-datacenter attitude, engineered faster
processors sooner to compete with RISC vendors, and didn't try to milk
as much $$$ as they thought they'd get away with.

Greed is more responsible for the rise of Unix, and the demise of
Digital more than any other aspect, IMHO.

