In article <34d5b1bb.0@isc-newsserver.isc.rit.edu>, "Alex Brin" 
<ajb8886@cs.rit.edu> writes:
|>I've spent many hours trying to track down the specifications for a Digital
|>VR419-A3 19" monitor I was given, to no avail.  All the fixed-sync monitor
|>databases I've tried have plenty of Digital monitors listed, but none that
|>match this one.

|>I'm very interested in trying to find the timing specs for this monitor, and
|>particularly so for the cabling information - I'd definitely like to try
|>driving this with a PC, if I could.  (I'm well aware that driving a
|>fixed-sync monitor with a PC gives me just one working resolution e.g.
|>1024*768, or 1152*864, or 1280*1024 - that's fine for the purposes I have in
|>mind.)

The VR419-A3 is a monochrome monitor with a fixed resolution of 1536x1152,
a fixed horizontal frequency of 86Khz and vertical refresh rate of 72Hz.
The three inputs are video, horizontal sync and vertical sync. The video
input is non-composite, RS343, DC coupled.  The -A3 suffix indicates
240VAC power input, though it might be switchable.  It was used on some
special-purpose VAX workstation, I believe. The larger connector is the
video input.

I would suggest forgetting the idea of using it on a PC of any type.
