David T. Wang wrote:
> Well, if they can deliver 100 Specint95, that would probably blow the
> doors off of anything, Merced, Power, MIPs, Sparc, or whatever. Still,
> that is a 5 fold performance increase in 24 months.  I remain slightly
> skeptical.

Such performances are only really necessary for CPU intensive
applications such as rendering et scientific stuff. But for average 
day to day use, many companies tend to split the loads amongst many PCs
(each running their own application/server). This is partly due to the
basic nature of operating systems of PCs and their apps which make it
too difficult to run multiple different apps on the same box.

So the end result is that even if DEC/COMPAQ can produce a 10gHz chip,
what counts is whether a single 10gHz machine costs more than buying
X numbers of commodity PCs to fill your needs.

And since many companies need to have multiple smaller PCs to do the
job, making one super fast chip doesn't gain much, unless it is priced
compatitively to inferior chips (eg: basically the same price, and ytou
get more).

Historically, Digital may have been competitive in the price/performance
arena, but not in price alone.



