match this one.

The monitor's BNC connectors seem rather unusual to me.  Three connectors in
a triangular arrangement, but two are BNC-type, and one is a coax-screw on
type (larger than the coax screws used on TV cables.)

The three connectors aren't labeled as RGB, but instead have icons next to
them.  The two BNC connectors have icons that resemble a monitor screen with
vertical and horizontal arrows, respectively; I'd assume that indicates
V-sync and H-sync.  The screw-on BNC connector has a blank monitor icon,
however.

Other details from the sticker on the back:
Digital model number: VR419-A3
serial number: AB930052DK
stamped: July 1989
Made in Alburquerque, NM.

The front of the monitor actually has a Kodak faceplate, but no information
to be found there either.

I'm very interested in trying to find the timing specs for this monitor, and
particularly so for the cabling information - I'd definitely like to try
driving this with a PC, if I could.  (I'm well aware that driving a
fixed-sync monitor with a PC gives me just one working resolution e.g.
1024*768, or 1152*864, or 1280*1024 - that's fine for the purposes I have in
mind.)

Any information would be much appreciated.  I've already tried all the
fixed-sync monitor FAQ's and databases I could find on the web - they all
have plenty of Digital monitors listed, but none of them have this part
number.

