In comp.sys.dec SpamTrap@[127.0.0.1] wrote:
> 	Maybe it's just me, and maybe I don't know the DEC parts market that
> well, but that seems awfully high no matter what. I paid about 1/4 of
> that, including truck rental, for my entire MicroVAX collection!

  It's very similar to coin collecting and other soft-demand markets - if
you're selling, everybody tells you what you have is junk and isn't worth
much (if anything at all), but if you're buying it's an "unusual item with
limited supply in the marketplace" or some such BS. Sometimes you can catch
these guys at it by offering to sell one, then saying "so, what if I wanted
to buy one". They'll sometimes be to embarassed to quote their usual full
price.

  However, this isn't just because they want to gouge you price-wise - there
are a number of reasons for this. First is the fact that their commercial
customers (who make up the majority of their business, not hobbyists) are
willing to pay those amounts. Secondly, if they have one oddball item they
probably have lots of other oddball items and need to have the space to 
store them all, as well as the ability to test and repair them (after all,
they're offering you some sort of warranty, even if it's only 30 days).

  Your best bet is to look in these newsgroups for announcements of stuff
being discarded, or ask if anyone is throwing stuff away or has parts to
trade.

